export const BATCH = [
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5000", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A 68-year-old male presents with substernal chest pressure radiating to the left arm. The 12-lead shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?",
  choices:["Left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"ST elevation in the inferior leads (II, III, aVF) reflects injury to the inferior wall, supplied in most patients by the right coronary artery. The LAD supplies the anterior/septal wall (V1-V4), the circumflex supplies the lateral wall (I, aVL, V5-V6), and left main occlusion typically produces widespread ST elevation with hemodynamic collapse, not isolated inferior changes." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5001", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with an inferior STEMI develops hypotension after receiving sublingual nitroglycerin. What is the most likely explanation?",
  choices:["An allergic reaction to nitroglycerin","Right ventricular infarction with preload dependence","Reflex bradycardia from vagal stimulation","Coincidental unrelated hypovolemia"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Inferior STEMIs frequently involve the right ventricle, which becomes highly preload-dependent. Nitroglycerin's venodilation reduces preload, causing profound hypotension in RV infarction. This is why a right-sided 12-lead should be obtained before giving nitrates in suspected inferior MI. The other options do not explain this specific, well-documented interaction." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5002", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Which ECG finding is most specific for Wellens syndrome, indicating critical proximal LAD stenosis?",
  choices:["Diffuse ST depression in all leads","Deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in V2-V3 with minimal or no ST elevation","New left bundle branch block","Peaked T waves in the precordial leads"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Wellens syndrome is characterized by symmetric, deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in the anterior precordial leads (especially V2-V3) during a pain-free interval, indicating critical proximal LAD stenosis at high risk for imminent anterior MI. Diffuse ST depression suggests subendocardial ischemia broadly; new LBBB and peaked T waves are different findings entirely." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5003", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"generate_solutions", itemType:"multiple_response",
  question:"Which of the following are contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy in a patient with STEMI symptom onset 90 minutes ago? (Select 3.)",
  choices:["History of hemorrhagic stroke at any time","Active internal bleeding","Blood pressure of 210/130 mmHg on arrival","Age greater than 65 years","Prior uncomplicated peptic ulcer disease 10 years ago"],
  correctIndices:[0,1,2],
  explanation:"Absolute or major relative contraindications to fibrinolytics include any prior hemorrhagic stroke, active internal bleeding, and severe uncontrolled hypertension (generally >180/110), all of which markedly raise bleeding risk. Age alone above 65 is not an absolute contraindication, though it is a risk factor. A remote, uncomplicated peptic ulcer many years in the past is not considered a current contraindication." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5004", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the primary mechanism by which amiodarone exerts its antiarrhythmic effect?",
  choices:["Pure beta-adrenergic blockade","Predominantly potassium channel blockade prolonging repolarization, with additional sodium, calcium, and beta-blocking properties","Selective sodium channel blockade only","Calcium channel blockade only"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Amiodarone is classified as a Class III antiarrhythmic because its dominant effect is potassium channel blockade, prolonging the action potential duration and refractory period, but it also has sodium channel blocking, calcium channel blocking, and non-competitive beta-blocking properties, making it a broad-spectrum agent rather than a pure single-class drug." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5005", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues", itemType:"multiple_choice", graphic:{kind:"ecg", rhythm:"VT", alt:"Lead II ECG rhythm strip."},
  question:"A hemodynamically stable 58-year-old with a history of prior myocardial infarction presents with the wide-complex tachycardia shown. This strip is schematic. Which finding, when seen on a full 12-lead in a wide-complex tachycardia like this, is most specific for ventricular tachycardia rather than SVT with aberrancy?",
  choices:["Heart rate greater than 150 bpm","QRS duration greater than 120 ms","AV dissociation with capture beats","Regular R-R interval"],
  answerIndex:2,
  explanation:"AV dissociation with fusion or capture beats is highly specific for ventricular tachycardia, since it demonstrates the atria and ventricles are depolarizing independently, which does not occur in SVT with aberrant conduction where the atria drive the rhythm. Rate above 150, QRS widening, and regularity can all occur in both VT and aberrantly conducted SVT and are not specific." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5006", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A stable patient is in monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a pulse. According to current ACLS guidance, which of the following is an appropriate first-line pharmacologic treatment?",
  choices:["Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV push","Procainamide or amiodarone infusion","Atropine 1 mg IV","High-dose epinephrine bolus"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"For stable monomorphic VT, procainamide, amiodarone, or sotalol are recommended antiarrhythmic options. Adenosine may be considered only for a truly stable, regular, monomorphic wide-complex tachycardia where SVT with aberrancy cannot be excluded, but it is not first-line therapy for confirmed VT. Atropine treats bradycardia, and epinephrine is reserved for pulseless arrest." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5007", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Torsades de pointes is most classically associated with which underlying electrolyte or ECG abnormality?",
  choices:["Hyperkalemia","A prolonged QT interval","A shortened PR interval","Peaked T waves"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Torsades de pointes is a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that arises specifically in the setting of a prolonged QT interval, whether congenital or acquired (drugs, hypomagnesemia, hypokalemia). Hyperkalemia and peaked T waves are associated with different rhythm disturbances, and a shortened PR interval is unrelated." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5008", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient is found in torsades de pointes with a pulse but is becoming hemodynamically unstable. What is the most appropriate immediate pharmacologic treatment?",
  choices:["Amiodarone 300 mg IV push","Magnesium sulfate 1-2 g IV over several minutes","Lidocaine 1.5 mg/kg IV","Calcium chloride 1 g IV"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Magnesium sulfate is the treatment of choice for torsades de pointes because it stabilizes the myocardial membrane and helps terminate the arrhythmia regardless of serum magnesium level. Amiodarone can, in some cases, further prolong the QT interval and worsen torsades. Lidocaine and calcium are not first-line for this specific rhythm." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5009", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues", itemType:"options_table",
  question:"For each ECG finding below, classify whether it is most consistent with hyperkalemia or hypokalemia.",
  options:["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia"],
  rows:[
    { id:"row1", finding:"Peaked, narrow T waves with a shortened QT interval", correctOptionIndex:0 },
    { id:"row2", finding:"Flattened T waves with a prominent U wave", correctOptionIndex:1 },
    { id:"row3", finding:"Widened QRS progressing toward a sine-wave pattern", correctOptionIndex:0 },
    { id:"row4", finding:"ST-segment depression with U waves that can mimic a prolonged QT", correctOptionIndex:1 }
  ],
  explanation:"Hyperkalemia classically progresses from peaked T waves to PR prolongation, QRS widening, and eventually a sine-wave pattern preceding arrest. Hypokalemia classically produces flattened T waves, prominent U waves, and ST depression, which can be mistaken for a prolonged QT interval since the U wave merges with the T wave." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5010", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A dialysis patient presents with peaked T waves, a widened QRS, and suspected severe hyperkalemia. Which medication stabilizes the cardiac membrane most rapidly, buying time for definitive treatment?",
  choices:["Sodium bicarbonate","Albuterol nebulizer","Calcium chloride or calcium gluconate","Insulin with dextrose"],
  answerIndex:2,
  explanation:"Calcium directly antagonizes the effect of potassium at the myocardial cell membrane, rapidly stabilizing cardiac conduction within minutes, though it does not lower the serum potassium level itself. Insulin/dextrose, albuterol, and bicarbonate all work by shifting potassium intracellularly, which does lower the effective serum level but takes longer to act and does not directly protect the myocardium." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5011", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the mechanism by which albuterol lowers serum potassium in the treatment of hyperkalemia?",
  choices:["It increases renal potassium excretion","It stimulates beta-2 receptors, driving potassium intracellularly via the Na-K-ATPase pump","It binds potassium in the gut for fecal excretion","It directly antagonizes potassium at the cell membrane"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Beta-2 agonism stimulates the Na-K-ATPase pump, shifting potassium from the extracellular space into cells, temporarily lowering serum potassium. It does not increase excretion (that is the role of diuretics, dialysis, or potassium binders) and does not stabilize the membrane the way calcium does." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5012", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"define_hypothesis",
  question:"A 45-year-old presents with severe, crushing chest pain radiating to the back described as tearing in quality, with unequal blood pressures between the two arms. Which diagnosis should be strongly suspected?",
  choices:["Acute myocardial infarction","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Costochondritis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Tearing or ripping chest/back pain with a pulse or blood pressure differential between extremities is a classic presentation of aortic dissection. This distinction matters clinically because nitroglycerin and thrombolytics, appropriate for ACS, could be harmful in dissection, and management instead focuses on blood pressure and heart rate control." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5013", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"In a hemodynamically stable patient with suspected aortic dissection and severe hypertension, which class of medication is generally preferred to control heart rate and blood pressure prior to considering vasodilators?",
  choices:["Beta blockers such as esmolol or labetalol","Calcium channel blocker dihydropyridines alone","Pure vasodilators such as nitroprusside alone","Loop diuretics"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Beta blockade is generally initiated first in aortic dissection to reduce heart rate and the force of left ventricular ejection (dP/dt), which reduces shear stress on the aortic wall. Vasodilators used alone, without prior beta blockade, can cause reflex tachycardia and increased shear stress, potentially worsening the dissection. Diuretics have no role in this acute presentation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5014", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of heart failure presents with progressive dyspnea, bilateral crackles to the mid-lung fields, jugular venous distension, and pink frothy sputum. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Spontaneous pneumothorax","Pulmonary embolism","Community-acquired pneumonia"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"The combination of bilateral crackles, JVD, and pink frothy sputum in a patient with a heart failure history is classic for acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema from elevated left ventricular filling pressures backing up into the pulmonary circulation. Pneumothorax typically presents with unilateral absent breath sounds, PE with sudden dyspnea and often clear lungs, and pneumonia typically with fever and focal findings." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5015", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A hypertensive patient in acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema is in significant respiratory distress but maintaining adequate mentation and respiratory effort. Which intervention is most appropriate as an early treatment priority?",
  choices:["Immediate endotracheal intubation","Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (CPAP) with nitroglycerin","High-dose furosemide alone with no other intervention","Aggressive fluid bolus to improve preload"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"CPAP reduces preload and afterload, recruits collapsed alveoli, and improves oxygenation, and combined with nitroglycerin for its preload/afterload-reducing venodilation and vasodilation, is first-line for a patient who is still able to protect their airway and breathe. Intubation is reserved for those who fail noninvasive support or cannot protect their airway. Fluid boluses would worsen pulmonary edema in this fluid-overloaded state." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5016", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the primary hemodynamic effect of nitroglycerin at low to moderate doses that makes it useful in cardiogenic pulmonary edema?",
  choices:["Primarily arterial vasoconstriction","Primarily venodilation, reducing cardiac preload","Positive inotropy, increasing contractility","Direct diuretic effect on the kidneys"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"At low to moderate doses, nitroglycerin predominantly causes venodilation, which reduces venous return (preload) to the heart, decreasing pulmonary congestion. At higher doses it also produces some arterial vasodilation, reducing afterload. It has no direct inotropic or diuretic effect." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5017", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"generate_solutions",
  question:"A 55-year-old presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after a long-haul flight, with a heart rate of 128 and SpO2 of 89% on room air. Which condition should be highest on the differential?",
  choices:["Pulmonary embolism","Stable angina","GERD","Costochondritis"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Recent prolonged immobility (a long flight), acute pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, and hypoxia together strongly suggest pulmonary embolism from venous thromboembolism. The other conditions listed do not typically produce this combination of acute hypoxia and tachycardia following a known risk factor for DVT/PE." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5018", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with a suspected massive pulmonary embolism develops cardiac arrest with PEA. Which reversible cause should be specifically suspected and considered for prehospital treatment?",
  choices:["Hypoglycemia treated with dextrose","Obstructive shock from PE, potentially benefiting from fibrinolytics per local protocol","Tension pneumothorax requiring needle decompression","Hyperkalemia treated with calcium"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Massive PE causing obstructive shock and PEA arrest is one of the H's and T's (thrombosis - pulmonary). Some EMS systems have protocols allowing prehospital fibrinolytic administration in this specific, witnessed scenario. Needle decompression treats tension pneumothorax, a different obstructive cause; the other options do not address the underlying PE." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5019", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Which finding on a 12-lead ECG, though nonspecific, is classically associated with acute pulmonary embolism?",
  choices:["Diffuse ST elevation in all leads","S1Q3T3 pattern (S wave in I, Q wave and inverted T wave in III)","Delta waves","Osborn (J) waves"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The S1Q3T3 pattern reflects acute right heart strain and is a classic, though insensitive and nonspecific, ECG finding in pulmonary embolism. Diffuse ST elevation suggests pericarditis or STEMI, delta waves suggest WPW, and Osborn waves are associated with hypothermia." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5020", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 32-year-old presents with sudden severe headache described as 'the worst headache of my life,' photophobia, and neck stiffness, with no history of trauma. Which diagnosis must be considered a priority?",
  choices:["Tension headache","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Sinusitis","Migraine without aura"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A sudden, severe, thunderclap headache described as the worst of the patient's life, especially with neck stiffness and photophobia, is the classic presentation of subarachnoid hemorrhage, most often from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm, and requires urgent recognition and transport. The other conditions do not typically produce this abrupt, maximal-intensity-at-onset pattern." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5021", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with sudden right-sided facial droop, right arm weakness, and expressive aphasia that began 40 minutes ago. Vitals are stable. What is the most appropriate immediate priority?",
  choices:["Administer aspirin immediately in the field","Rapidly determine last known well time and transport to an appropriate stroke-capable facility","Wait at the scene until symptoms fully resolve before transporting","Administer high-dose dextrose regardless of blood glucose"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Time is the critical factor in acute stroke care, since fibrinolytics and mechanical thrombectomy are time-sensitive. Establishing an accurate last known well time and rapid transport to a stroke center is the priority. Aspirin is not given prehospital for suspected stroke, since hemorrhagic stroke cannot be excluded without imaging, and unnecessary delay at the scene is harmful." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5022", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Why is prehospital blood glucose measurement essential in any patient presenting with stroke-like symptoms?",
  choices:["Elevated glucose confirms the diagnosis of stroke","Hypoglycemia is a common stroke mimic that is rapidly reversible with treatment","Hyperglycemia is the leading cause of ischemic stroke","Glucose level determines eligibility for fibrinolytics only"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Hypoglycemia can produce focal neurological deficits that closely mimic stroke, and it is rapidly correctable, so it must always be excluded before assuming a stroke diagnosis. Glucose does not confirm stroke, and while glucose control matters for outcomes and fibrinolytic eligibility, the primary prehospital reason to check it is to rule out a reversible mimic." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5023", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient in generalized tonic-clonic seizure activity for the past 6 minutes has not stopped despite one dose of midazolam. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  choices:["Wait an additional 10 minutes before further intervention","Administer a second dose of a benzodiazepine per protocol and prepare for advanced airway management if it continues","Restrain the patient forcefully to prevent injury and do nothing further","Administer high-flow oxygen only and transport without further medication"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Status epilepticus, generally defined as continuous seizure activity for 5 minutes or more or recurrent seizures without return to baseline, requires prompt escalation of therapy, typically a repeat benzodiazepine dose per protocol, with preparation for advanced airway management if seizures persist, since prolonged seizure activity causes ongoing cerebral hypoxia and metabolic demand." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5024", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the primary mechanism of action of benzodiazepines such as midazolam in terminating seizure activity?",
  choices:["Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels","Enhancing GABA-mediated chloride channel opening, increasing inhibitory neurotransmission","Blocking NMDA glutamate receptors","Depleting presynaptic calcium stores"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Benzodiazepines bind to the GABA-A receptor complex, enhancing the effect of GABA (the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter) by increasing chloride channel opening frequency, which hyperpolarizes neurons and raises the seizure threshold. Sodium channel blockade describes drugs like phenytoin; NMDA antagonism describes ketamine." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5025", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A diabetic patient is found confused, diaphoretic, and tachycardic with a blood glucose of 38 mg/dL. The patient is able to swallow safely. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?",
  choices:["IV/IO dextrose only","Oral glucose gel or glucose-containing food/drink if the patient can safely swallow","IM glucagon as the first choice regardless of airway status","Withhold treatment until hospital arrival for lab confirmation"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In a hypoglycemic patient who can protect their airway and swallow safely, oral glucose is a reasonable and effective first step. IV dextrose is preferred when IV access is already established or the patient cannot safely take oral glucose; IM glucagon is generally reserved for when IV access cannot be obtained. Withholding treatment for a symptomatic hypoglycemic patient is inappropriate and dangerous." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5026", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the mechanism of action of glucagon in treating hypoglycemia?",
  choices:["It directly provides glucose to the bloodstream","It stimulates hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, releasing stored glucose","It suppresses insulin secretion from the pancreas","It increases renal glucose reabsorption"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Glucagon stimulates the liver to break down stored glycogen (glycogenolysis) and produce new glucose (gluconeogenesis), raising blood glucose indirectly. It does not itself contain glucose, which is why it is less effective in patients with depleted glycogen stores, such as chronic alcoholics or those who are malnourished." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5027", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A type 1 diabetic presents with Kussmaul respirations, fruity breath odor, dry mucous membranes, and a blood glucose of 480 mg/dL. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Insulin overdose","Alcoholic ketoacidosis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing compensating for metabolic acidosis), fruity (acetone) breath odor, and significant hyperglycemia in a type 1 diabetic are classic for diabetic ketoacidosis, driven by absolute insulin deficiency leading to ketone production and metabolic acidosis. HHS typically occurs in type 2 diabetics with even higher glucose but without significant ketosis or acidosis." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5028", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the priority prehospital treatment for a patient in diabetic ketoacidosis with signs of significant dehydration?",
  choices:["Isotonic IV fluid resuscitation (normal saline)","Immediate administration of subcutaneous insulin","Sodium bicarbonate to correct the acidosis","Withholding fluids until arrival at the hospital"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Isotonic fluid resuscitation is the priority in DKA, addressing the significant volume depletion from osmotic diuresis and helping to dilute glucose and improve perfusion. Insulin is typically administered in the hospital setting with careful potassium monitoring, since insulin drives potassium intracellularly and can precipitate dangerous hypokalemia; bicarbonate is generally not recommended in the field for DKA." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5029", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Why does diabetic ketoacidosis typically present with a normal or even elevated measured serum potassium despite significant total body potassium depletion?",
  choices:["DKA causes increased renal potassium reabsorption","Acidosis drives potassium out of cells into the extracellular space, masking total body depletion","Insulin deficiency directly increases serum potassium production","Ketones bind potassium, artificially raising lab values"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In acidosis, hydrogen ions shift into cells in exchange for potassium moving into the extracellular (and therefore measured serum) compartment, masking a true total body potassium deficit caused by osmotic diuresis. This is why insulin treatment, which shifts potassium back intracellularly, can precipitate severe hypokalemia if not carefully monitored and replaced." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5030", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues", itemType:"multiple_response",
  question:"Which of the following findings are consistent with anaphylaxis rather than a mild localized allergic reaction? (Select 2.)",
  choices:["Isolated hives on one forearm with no other symptoms","Stridor and audible wheezing with hypotension","Angioedema of the lips and tongue with difficulty breathing","Mild itching at a insect bite site only","A single episode of sneezing"],
  correctIndices:[1,2],
  explanation:"Anaphylaxis involves systemic, multi-organ involvement, classically including airway compromise (stridor, wheezing), and often hypotension or angioedema affecting the airway. Isolated skin findings without systemic or airway involvement, or isolated minor symptoms like sneezing, represent a localized or mild reaction, not anaphylaxis." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5031", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the first-line treatment for a patient in anaphylaxis with hypotension and respiratory distress?",
  choices:["Diphenhydramine IV/IM as the first-line agent","Intramuscular epinephrine 1:1,000 in the anterolateral thigh","Albuterol nebulizer alone","High-dose corticosteroids alone as the first intervention"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Epinephrine IM in the anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis) is the first-line, life-saving treatment for anaphylaxis, providing alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction to counteract hypotension and beta-2 mediated bronchodilation. Antihistamines, steroids, and bronchodilators are useful adjuncts but do not replace epinephrine as the immediate priority, since they act more slowly and do not reverse the core mast-cell-mediated shock physiology." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5032", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Why is epinephrine given intramuscularly rather than subcutaneously in the treatment of anaphylaxis?",
  choices:["IM injection is more painful and therefore more effective","IM injection produces faster, more reliable peak plasma concentrations, especially in a hypoperfused patient","Subcutaneous injection is contraindicated in all patients","IM injection avoids the need for repeat dosing"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"IM injection, particularly into the vastus lateralis, achieves faster and more reliable absorption than subcutaneous injection, which is especially important in anaphylaxis where peripheral vasoconstriction and hypoperfusion can further delay subcutaneous absorption. Repeat dosing may still be needed with either route if symptoms persist." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5033", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with fever, hypotension, tachycardia, and altered mental status following several days of a productive cough. Lactate is elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Simple community-acquired pneumonia without complication","Septic shock secondary to pneumonia","Cardiogenic shock","Anaphylactic shock"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The combination of a likely infectious source (productive cough suggesting pneumonia), fever, hypotension, tachycardia, altered mentation, and elevated lactate meets criteria consistent with septic shock, a distributive shock state driven by the systemic inflammatory response to infection. The other options do not fit this infectious, distributive presentation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5034", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the priority prehospital intervention for a patient in septic shock with hypotension?",
  choices:["Broad-spectrum antibiotics administered in the field","Isotonic crystalloid fluid resuscitation and early identification/transport to an appropriate facility","Vasopressors as the sole initial treatment before any fluids","Withholding fluids to avoid pulmonary edema"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Prehospital management of septic shock centers on early recognition, isotonic fluid resuscitation to improve perfusion, and rapid transport, since early goal-directed therapy improves outcomes in sepsis. Antibiotics are typically hospital-initiated (though some advanced systems now carry them). Vasopressors are generally reserved for hypotension refractory to adequate fluid resuscitation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5035", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Norepinephrine is generally preferred over dopamine as a first-line vasopressor in septic shock because:",
  choices:["Norepinephrine has no cardiac effects at all","Norepinephrine is associated with lower rates of arrhythmia and mortality compared to dopamine in septic shock trials","Dopamine cannot be given peripherally under any circumstance","Norepinephrine has no vasoconstrictive properties"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Clinical trials comparing norepinephrine and dopamine in septic shock have shown that dopamine is associated with higher rates of arrhythmias and, in some analyses, higher mortality, leading current guidelines to recommend norepinephrine as the first-line vasopressor. Norepinephrine does have some beta-1 activity and is a potent alpha-1 vasoconstrictor." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5036", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe respiratory distress, a barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing, and diminished breath sounds bilaterally, with a known history of long-term smoking. Oxygen saturation is 88%. What is a key consideration when administering supplemental oxygen to this patient?",
  choices:["Oxygen should never be given to COPD patients under any circumstances","Titrate oxygen to an appropriate target saturation rather than withholding it, while monitoring for hypoventilation","High-flow 100% oxygen should always be given regardless of saturation","Oxygen therapy has no risk in COPD patients"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"While some COPD patients who chronically retain CO2 may rely more on hypoxic respiratory drive, the priority in significant hypoxia is still to correct it, but oxygen should be titrated to an appropriate target (commonly around 88-92% in known chronic retainers) rather than either withheld inappropriately or given at unnecessarily high flow, monitoring for signs of hypoventilation or worsening mental status." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5037", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the primary mechanism by which ipratropium bromide provides bronchodilation in COPD and asthma exacerbations?",
  choices:["Beta-2 adrenergic receptor agonism","Anticholinergic blockade of muscarinic receptors, reducing bronchoconstriction and secretions","Direct smooth muscle relaxation via calcium channel blockade","Mast cell stabilization"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Ipratropium is an anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) agent that blocks acetylcholine's bronchoconstrictive effect at muscarinic receptors in the airway smooth muscle, also reducing mucus secretion. This is a distinct mechanism from beta-2 agonists like albuterol, which is why the two are frequently combined for synergistic effect." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5038", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"evaluation",
  question:"A patient in status asthmaticus who was initially wheezing loudly now has diminished breath sounds and minimal audible wheezing, with worsening fatigue. What does this change most likely represent?",
  choices:["Significant clinical improvement","Impending respiratory failure due to severely reduced air movement","A normal resolution of the exacerbation","An allergic reaction to the treatment given"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The disappearance of wheezing in a severe asthma exacerbation, especially with fatigue, is an ominous sign indicating that air movement has become so poor that turbulent airflow is no longer generating audible wheeze, a hallmark of impending respiratory failure requiring aggressive intervention, not a sign of improvement." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5039", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with severe asthma exacerbation is not improving adequately with nebulized albuterol and ipratropium. Which medication is appropriate as an adjunct with bronchodilatory and membrane-stabilizing properties?",
  choices:["Naloxone","Magnesium sulfate IV","Atropine IV","Furosemide IV"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"IV magnesium sulfate acts as a smooth muscle relaxant and has been shown to provide additional bronchodilation in severe asthma exacerbations refractory to standard beta-agonist and anticholinergic therapy. The other medications listed do not have a bronchodilatory role in asthma management." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5040", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient presents with a suspected opioid overdose: pinpoint pupils, respiratory rate of 4 breaths per minute, and unresponsiveness. What is the priority action?",
  choices:["Immediate administration of naloxone before addressing the airway","Prioritize ventilatory support (BVM) while preparing naloxone, since airway/breathing takes precedence","Withhold ventilation until naloxone takes full effect","Perform chest compressions immediately regardless of pulse status"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Airway and breathing take priority; positive pressure ventilation should be initiated for severe respiratory depression while naloxone is prepared and administered, since naloxone's onset is not instantaneous and adequate oxygenation/ventilation must not be delayed. Chest compressions are inappropriate if a pulse is present." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5041", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the mechanism of action of naloxone in reversing opioid overdose?",
  choices:["It is a partial opioid agonist that mimics opioid effects","It is a competitive opioid receptor antagonist that displaces opioids from mu receptors","It stimulates the medullary respiratory center directly","It chemically neutralizes opioids in the bloodstream"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Naloxone is a competitive antagonist at opioid receptors, particularly the mu receptor, displacing the opioid agonist and reversing its effects, including respiratory depression. It has no agonist activity of its own and does not chemically neutralize the opioid or directly stimulate the respiratory center." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5042", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"After naloxone administration for opioid overdose involving a long-acting opioid, a patient becomes fully alert with normal vital signs. What is an important consideration for ongoing management?",
  choices:["The patient no longer requires monitoring or transport","Naloxone's duration of action may be shorter than the opioid's, risking re-sedation, so continued monitoring and possibly repeat dosing are needed","A second dose of naloxone should never be given","The patient should be immediately discharged from care regardless of the opioid involved"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Naloxone has a relatively short duration of action (roughly 30-90 minutes) compared to many opioids, particularly long-acting or high-potency formulations, so a patient can re-sedate as the naloxone wears off while the opioid remains active, necessitating ongoing monitoring, transport, and potential repeat dosing." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5043", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with agitation, hyperthermia, dilated pupils, tachycardia, and hypertension after suspected cocaine use. Which class of medication should generally be avoided due to the risk of unopposed alpha stimulation?",
  choices:["Benzodiazepines","Non-selective beta blockers","Isotonic IV fluids","Active cooling measures"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Non-selective beta blockers can block beta-2-mediated vasodilation while leaving alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed, potentially worsening hypertension and coronary vasospasm in sympathomimetic toxicity such as cocaine use. Benzodiazepines are the preferred first-line agent for agitation and cardiovascular effects in this setting." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5044", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the primary toxic mechanism of tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose that produces widened QRS complexes on ECG?",
  choices:["Potassium channel blockade","Fast sodium channel blockade in cardiac tissue","Beta-adrenergic receptor blockade","Calcium channel blockade"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"TCAs block fast sodium channels in the myocardium, slowing phase 0 depolarization and widening the QRS complex, which correlates with seizure and arrhythmia risk. This sodium channel blocking property is analogous to Class IA/IC antiarrhythmic effects and is the basis for treating TCA cardiotoxicity with sodium bicarbonate." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5045", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with suspected tricyclic antidepressant overdose has a QRS duration of 140 ms on the monitor. What is the appropriate prehospital treatment?",
  choices:["Sodium bicarbonate IV bolus","Calcium chloride IV","Flumazenil IV","Physostigmine IV"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Sodium bicarbonate is the treatment of choice for TCA-induced cardiotoxicity with QRS widening, working by increasing extracellular sodium concentration to overcome sodium channel blockade and by alkalinizing the blood, which reduces the drug's affinity for the sodium channel. Flumazenil is contraindicated in mixed overdoses risking seizures; physostigmine is not indicated for TCA toxicity." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5046", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with bradycardia, hypotension, and altered mental status after a suspected beta-blocker overdose, unresponsive to standard atropine dosing. Which medications are useful adjuncts specific to beta-blocker toxicity?",
  choices:["Naloxone and flumazenil","Glucagon and high-dose insulin/dextrose therapy","Activated charcoal alone with no other intervention","Diphenhydramine and epinephrine only"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Glucagon bypasses the blocked beta receptor to activate adenylate cyclase directly, improving heart rate and contractility, and is a classic antidote for beta-blocker overdose. High-dose insulin/dextrose (HIE) therapy is also increasingly used for refractory beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker toxicity, improving myocardial glucose utilization and contractility. Naloxone/flumazenil address opioid/benzodiazepine toxicity, not beta-blocker overdose." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5047", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with a calcium channel blocker overdose presents with profound bradycardia and hypotension refractory to fluids and atropine. Which medication directly addresses the underlying mechanism of toxicity?",
  choices:["Calcium chloride IV","Naloxone IV","Diphenhydramine IV","Ondansetron IV"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Calcium chloride (or gluconate) can help overcome the calcium channel blockade by increasing extracellular calcium available to enter cells through unblocked channels, improving cardiac contractility and vascular tone, directly countering the mechanism of toxicity. The other medications listed have no specific role in calcium channel blocker overdose." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5048", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with excessive salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhea, GI distress, and emesis following suspected organophosphate pesticide exposure, with significant bronchorrhea and bradycardia. What is the underlying mechanism of this toxidrome?",
  choices:["Excessive sympathetic nervous system stimulation","Acetylcholinesterase inhibition causing excess acetylcholine at muscarinic and nicotinic receptors","Direct opioid receptor agonism","GABA receptor antagonism"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that normally breaks down acetylcholine, leading to excess acetylcholine accumulation at both muscarinic receptors (producing the SLUDGE/DUMBELS symptoms) and nicotinic receptors (producing muscle fasciculations and weakness). This is the classic cholinergic toxidrome." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5049", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the primary prehospital pharmacologic treatment for severe organophosphate poisoning with significant bronchorrhea and bradycardia?",
  choices:["High-dose atropine, titrated to drying of secretions","Naloxone","Beta blockers to control heart rate","Diphenhydramine"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Atropine competitively blocks the excess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, reversing bronchorrhea, bradycardia, and excessive secretions; doses required in severe organophosphate poisoning can be very high and are titrated to drying of secretions rather than a fixed dose. Pralidoxime (2-PAM) may also be used to reactivate acetylcholinesterase where available, but atropine addresses the immediate life threats first." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5050", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient rescued from a house fire presents with altered mental status, headache, and a pulse oximetry reading of 99% on room air, but appears cyanotic and is in significant distress. What should be suspected?",
  choices:["The pulse oximetry reading is accurate and reassuring","Carbon monoxide poisoning, since pulse oximetry cannot distinguish carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin","The patient is malingering","Simple anxiety with no toxic exposure"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Standard pulse oximetry cannot differentiate carboxyhemoglobin (CO bound to hemoglobin) from oxyhemoglobin, so it can read falsely normal or high in carbon monoxide poisoning despite significantly impaired oxygen-carrying capacity. Clinical suspicion based on exposure history and symptoms, along with co-oximetry if available, is essential rather than relying on standard SpO2." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5051", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the definitive prehospital treatment for suspected significant carbon monoxide poisoning?",
  choices:["Room air only, since supplemental oxygen has no benefit","High-flow 100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask, which accelerates carboxyhemoglobin dissociation","Naloxone administration","Withholding oxygen to avoid oxygen toxicity"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"High-flow 100% oxygen significantly shortens the half-life of carboxyhemoglobin compared to room air, accelerating displacement of carbon monoxide from hemoglobin and improving oxygen delivery to tissues. Hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be indicated for severe cases at the hospital, but high-flow oxygen is the immediate prehospital treatment." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5052", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient exposed to a house fire in an enclosed space presents with altered mental status, severe metabolic acidosis, and a normal pulse oximetry and PaO2 despite clear respiratory distress. Cyanide toxicity is suspected. Which finding supports this diagnosis over pure carbon monoxide poisoning?",
  choices:["A normal anion gap","Severe lactic acidosis out of proportion to hypoxia, since cyanide blocks cellular oxygen utilization at the mitochondrial level","A prolonged PR interval","Hypoglycemia alone"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Cyanide inhibits cytochrome c oxidase, blocking mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, so tissues cannot use oxygen even when it is delivered, causing severe lactic acidosis (a high anion gap metabolic acidosis) disproportionate to the degree of hypoxia. This is a distinguishing feature from pure carbon monoxide poisoning, which is a delivery problem rather than a cellular utilization problem." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5053", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the mechanism of action of hydroxocobalamin in treating cyanide toxicity?",
  choices:["It directly reverses acetylcholinesterase inhibition","It binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12), which is renally excreted","It stimulates beta-adrenergic receptors","It chelates lead from the bloodstream"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Hydroxocobalamin binds free cyanide directly, forming cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12), a nontoxic compound that is excreted in the urine, effectively removing cyanide from circulation and restoring mitochondrial oxygen utilization. It has become a preferred prehospital antidote due to its favorable safety profile compared to older cyanide antidote kit components." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5054", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with agitation, hyperthermia, tachycardia, hyperreflexia, and clonus, especially in the lower extremities, after recently starting an SSRI and adding tramadol for pain. What syndrome should be suspected?",
  choices:["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Anticholinergic toxidrome"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Serotonin syndrome results from excess serotonergic activity, classically from combining serotonergic medications (SSRIs with tramadol, MAOIs, or other serotonergic agents), producing the triad of neuromuscular hyperactivity (clonus, hyperreflexia, worse in the lower extremities), autonomic instability (hyperthermia, tachycardia), and altered mental status. NMS instead presents with lead-pipe rigidity and is linked to antipsychotic use, not serotonergic combinations." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5055", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe generalized muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, and altered mental status days after an increase in antipsychotic medication dose, without the clonus seen in serotonin syndrome. What should be suspected?",
  choices:["Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Simple heat exhaustion","Opioid withdrawal"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is caused by dopamine receptor antagonism (typically from antipsychotic medications), presenting with sustained, uniform 'lead-pipe' rigidity, hyperthermia, autonomic instability, and altered mental status, typically with a slower onset over days rather than the rapid onset of serotonin syndrome. The absence of clonus and the antipsychotic exposure history distinguish it from serotonin syndrome." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5056", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the primary prehospital management for both serotonin syndrome and neuroleptic malignant syndrome presenting with severe agitation and hyperthermia?",
  choices:["Physical restraint alone without medication","Benzodiazepines for agitation/muscle activity, plus active cooling measures, since no specific field antidote exists for either","Beta blockers as first-line treatment","Naloxone as the definitive treatment"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Prehospital management for both syndromes centers on supportive care: benzodiazepines to control agitation and reduce excess muscle activity/heat production, and active cooling for hyperthermia, since no specific antidote (such as cyproheptadine for serotonin syndrome or dantrolene for NMS) is typically carried in the field. Physical restraint alone without addressing agitation can worsen hyperthermia through continued muscular exertion." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5057", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe agitation, hyperthermia, and reportedly superhuman strength, tolerating repeated tasing or physical restraint attempts without apparent effect, then suddenly becomes calm and loses consciousness. What is the most likely diagnosis and immediate concern?",
  choices:["A benign panic attack resolving spontaneously","Excited delirium syndrome, with high risk of sudden cardiac arrest","Simple alcohol intoxication","A seizure with a normal postictal state"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Excited delirium (also called hyperactive delirium with severe agitation) presents with extreme agitation, hyperthermia, and apparent insensitivity to pain, often related to stimulant use or acute psychiatric decompensation, and carries a significant risk of sudden cardiac arrest, particularly around the point where the patient suddenly becomes calm, which should be treated as a medical emergency, not reassurance." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5058", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"Which prehospital approach is most appropriate for a patient in excited delirium syndrome?",
  choices:["Prolonged physical struggle to fully subdue the patient before any medication","Early chemical sedation, avoidance of prone restraint, and close cardiac/respiratory monitoring","No sedation of any kind, only physical restraint","Immediate defibrillation regardless of rhythm"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Early pharmacologic sedation (commonly with a benzodiazazepine or ketamine per local protocol), avoiding prolonged prone positioning and restraint (which can contribute to positional asphyxia), and close monitoring for cardiac arrest are the recommended approach, since prolonged physical struggle increases catecholamine surge, exertion, and hyperthermia, raising arrest risk." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5059", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of sickle cell disease presents with severe diffuse bone and joint pain, tachycardia, and mild fever. What is this presentation most consistent with?",
  choices:["A simple viral syndrome","Vaso-occlusive (sickle cell) crisis","Acute compartment syndrome","Rheumatoid arthritis flare"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Vaso-occlusive crisis occurs when sickled red blood cells obstruct microvascular blood flow, causing ischemic pain, classically in the bones, joints, chest, and abdomen, often accompanied by low-grade fever and tachycardia from pain and the underlying inflammatory process. This is the most common reason sickle cell patients seek emergency care." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5060", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What are the priority prehospital interventions for a patient in sickle cell vaso-occlusive crisis?",
  choices:["Aggressive diuresis and fluid restriction","IV fluid hydration, supplemental oxygen if hypoxic, and adequate analgesia","Withholding pain medication to avoid masking symptoms","Immediate blood transfusion in the field"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"IV hydration helps reduce blood viscosity and improve microvascular flow, supplemental oxygen addresses any hypoxia (which can worsen sickling), and adequate analgesia, often with opioids, is essential since vaso-occlusive crisis pain is severe and undertreatment is a recognized issue in sickle cell care. Blood transfusion is a hospital-based intervention for specific indications, not routine prehospital care." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5061", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A sickle cell patient presents with acute chest pain, hypoxia, fever, and a new infiltrate-type presentation with respiratory distress. What life-threatening complication should be suspected?",
  choices:["Simple pneumonia unrelated to sickle cell disease","Acute chest syndrome","Aortic dissection","Spontaneous pneumothorax"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Acute chest syndrome is a life-threatening complication of sickle cell disease involving pulmonary vaso-occlusion and/or infection, presenting with chest pain, fever, hypoxia, and respiratory distress, and requires aggressive supportive management, supplemental oxygen, and rapid transport, since it is a leading cause of death in sickle cell patients." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5062", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient found down outdoors in extreme heat presents with altered mental status, hot dry skin, and a core temperature of 105°F (40.6°C). What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Heat cramps","Heat exhaustion","Heat stroke","Simple dehydration"],
  answerIndex:2,
  explanation:"Heat stroke is defined by significantly elevated core body temperature (typically above 104°F/40°C) combined with central nervous system dysfunction (altered mental status, confusion, seizure, or coma), representing a failure of thermoregulation and constituting a true medical emergency requiring immediate cooling. Heat exhaustion typically presents with a lower temperature and normal mentation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5063", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the priority treatment for a patient in heat stroke?",
  choices:["Slow, gradual cooling over several hours","Rapid, aggressive cooling as quickly as possible, such as ice water immersion or evaporative cooling with misting and fanning","Administration of antipyretics such as acetaminophen","Withholding cooling until hospital arrival"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Rapid cooling is the single most important intervention in heat stroke, since the duration and degree of hyperthermia correlate directly with mortality and organ damage. Ice water immersion is the most effective method where feasible; evaporative cooling with misting and fanning is a practical prehospital alternative. Antipyretics like acetaminophen are ineffective for heat stroke, since the mechanism is not a hypothalamic set-point change as in fever." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5064", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient found in a cold environment presents with a core temperature of 88°F (31°C), bradycardia, and diminished level of consciousness. The cardiac monitor shows a J (Osborn) wave. What should guide handling of this patient?",
  choices:["Handle the patient aggressively, since cold myocardium is stable","Handle the patient gently, since a cold, irritable myocardium is at increased risk of ventricular fibrillation with rough movement","Perform vigorous chest compressions regardless of pulse status","Rapidly rewarm using hot water immersion"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Moderate to severe hypothermia significantly increases myocardial irritability, and rough handling or excessive movement can precipitate ventricular fibrillation, so gentle handling is essential. Rapid, aggressive rewarming (such as hot water immersion) is not recommended in the field due to risks of afterdrop and arrhythmia; passive and gentle active rewarming is preferred, and compressions are reserved for confirmed cardiac arrest." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5065", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What ECG finding is classically associated with hypothermia?",
  choices:["Delta wave","Osborn (J) wave, a positive deflection at the junction of the QRS complex and ST segment","U wave","Epsilon wave"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The Osborn (J) wave, a positive deflection or 'hump' at the J point (junction of the QRS complex and ST segment), is a classic ECG finding associated with hypothermia, and its prominence often correlates with the severity of hypothermia. Delta waves are seen in WPW, U waves in hypokalemia, and epsilon waves in arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5066", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"evaluation",
  question:"A severely hypothermic patient in cardiac arrest is found without a palpable pulse after a prolonged pulse check. What is an appropriate modification to standard resuscitation guidance in this scenario?",
  choices:["Declare death immediately regardless of core temperature","Consider prolonged resuscitation efforts, since hypothermia can be protective against hypoxic injury, and the adage 'not dead until warm and dead' applies","Withhold all resuscitation efforts in any hypothermic patient","Perform standard-duration resuscitation identical to a normothermic patient with no modification"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Hypothermia decreases cellular metabolic demand and can provide some protection against hypoxic-ischemic injury, so patients in severe hypothermic cardiac arrest may have a better chance of neurologically intact survival with prolonged resuscitation than a normothermic arrest patient, giving rise to the teaching that a hypothermic patient is 'not dead until warm and dead.' Local protocols and medical control guidance should be followed." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5067", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues", itemType:"drag_drop",
  question:"Categorize each finding as most consistent with heat exhaustion or heat stroke.",
  categories:[
    { id:"exhaustion", label:"Heat Exhaustion" },
    { id:"stroke", label:"Heat Stroke" }
  ],
  items:[
    { id:"item1", label:"Alert and oriented, profuse sweating, temperature 101°F", correctCategory:"exhaustion" },
    { id:"item2", label:"Confused and combative, hot dry or flushed skin, temperature 105°F", correctCategory:"stroke" },
    { id:"item3", label:"Nausea, weakness, normal mentation, moist skin", correctCategory:"exhaustion" },
    { id:"item4", label:"Seizure activity, core temperature markedly elevated, altered consciousness", correctCategory:"stroke" }
  ],
  explanation:"Heat exhaustion presents with normal mental status, sweating, and a mildly to moderately elevated temperature, representing a failure of adequate hydration/perfusion but preserved thermoregulation. Heat stroke represents failure of thermoregulation itself, with a markedly elevated core temperature and central nervous system dysfunction (confusion, seizure, coma) as defining features." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5068", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 30-year-old female at approximately 34 weeks gestation presents with severe headache, visual disturbances, epigastric pain, and a blood pressure of 168/112 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Gestational diabetes","Severe preeclampsia","Normal pregnancy discomfort","Braxton Hicks contractions"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"New-onset severe hypertension after 20 weeks gestation with symptoms such as headache, visual disturbances, or epigastric pain (suggesting hepatic involvement) is characteristic of severe preeclampsia, a hypertensive disorder of pregnancy that can progress to eclampsia (seizures) if untreated, and requires urgent evaluation and management." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5069", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A pregnant patient with severe preeclampsia begins having generalized tonic-clonic seizure activity (eclampsia). What is the medication of choice for both seizure treatment and prevention of recurrence?",
  choices:["Phenytoin IV","Magnesium sulfate IV","Lorazepam as the sole agent with no other treatment","Diazepam as first-line before considering any other agent"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Magnesium sulfate is the standard of care for both treating eclamptic seizures and preventing their recurrence, working through mechanisms including cerebral vasodilation and NMDA receptor antagonism, and is superior to benzodiazepines and phenytoin for this specific indication based on strong clinical evidence. Benzodiazepines may still be used as an adjunct if seizures are refractory to magnesium." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5070", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is a key sign of magnesium sulfate toxicity that must be monitored during treatment for preeclampsia/eclampsia?",
  choices:["Hyperreflexia","Loss of deep tendon reflexes, respiratory depression, and decreased level of consciousness","Tachycardia and hypertension","Increased urine output only"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Magnesium toxicity classically progresses from loss of deep tendon reflexes to respiratory depression and eventually cardiac arrest as serum levels rise, so deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, and mental status must be monitored during magnesium administration, with calcium gluconate available as an antidote if toxicity develops." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5071", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A third-trimester pregnant patient in a supine position on the stretcher becomes hypotensive and dizzy. What is the most likely cause and appropriate intervention?",
  choices:["Cardiogenic shock; administer a fluid bolus and vasopressors","Supine hypotensive syndrome from aortocaval compression; position the patient in the left lateral decubitus position or manually displace the uterus leftward","Anaphylaxis; administer epinephrine","Hypoglycemia; administer dextrose"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In late pregnancy, the gravid uterus can compress the inferior vena cava and aorta when the patient lies supine, reducing venous return and cardiac output, causing supine hypotensive syndrome. Positioning the patient in the left lateral decubitus position, or manually displacing the uterus to the left if immobilized, relieves this compression and is the appropriate first intervention." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5072", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A pregnant patient at 32 weeks presents with sudden-onset severe abdominal pain, a rigid/tender uterus, and dark vaginal bleeding. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Placenta previa","Placental abruption","Normal Braxton Hicks contractions","Round ligament pain"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Placental abruption, the premature separation of the placenta from the uterine wall, classically presents with sudden severe abdominal pain, a firm/rigid and tender uterus, and vaginal bleeding (which can sometimes be concealed behind the placenta), and is a life-threatening obstetric emergency for both mother and fetus. Placenta previa classically presents with painless bright red bleeding instead." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5073", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A pregnant patient at 30 weeks presents with painless, bright red vaginal bleeding. What diagnosis should be strongly suspected, and what examination should be avoided in the field?",
  choices:["Placental abruption; avoid oxygen administration","Placenta previa; avoid digital vaginal examination, which could provoke severe hemorrhage","Normal spotting; no special precautions needed","Ectopic pregnancy; avoid IV fluid administration"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Placenta previa, where the placenta partially or completely covers the cervical os, classically presents with painless bright red bleeding. Digital vaginal examination should be avoided in any pregnant patient with third-trimester bleeding until previa is excluded by ultrasound, since it can dislodge the placenta and precipitate life-threatening hemorrhage." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5074", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"During delivery, the umbilical cord is visualized protruding from the vaginal opening ahead of the presenting fetal part. What is this emergency, and what is the priority intervention?",
  choices:["Nuchal cord; clamp and cut the cord immediately","Umbilical cord prolapse; relieve pressure on the cord by elevating the presenting part and position the mother knee-chest or Trendelenburg, and transport rapidly","Normal delivery variant requiring no intervention","Placental abruption; apply fundal pressure"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Umbilical cord prolapse is an obstetric emergency because the presenting fetal part can compress the cord against the pelvis, cutting off fetal blood supply. The priority is to relieve pressure on the cord, typically by inserting a gloved hand to elevate the presenting part off the cord, positioning the mother in a knee-chest or steep Trendelenburg position, and transporting immediately without attempting to push the cord back in." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5075", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"During delivery, only a single leg is presenting through the vaginal opening (breech/limb presentation). What is the appropriate field management?",
  choices:["Attempt to manually reposition the fetus into a vertex position","Do not attempt delivery in the field; position the mother with hips elevated and provide immediate rapid transport to a facility capable of emergency cesarean delivery","Apply strong traction on the presenting limb to facilitate delivery","Perform an episiotomy immediately"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Breech and limb presentations carry a high risk of cord compression, entrapment, and fetal injury, and are generally not deliverable safely in the field. The appropriate management is to avoid manipulation, position the mother to reduce pressure on the presenting part (such as knees to chest or hips elevated), and transport emergently for cesarean delivery." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5076", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"After a normal vaginal delivery, the mother continues to have significant vaginal bleeding and the uterine fundus feels soft and boggy rather than firm. What is the most likely cause and appropriate intervention?",
  choices:["Retained placenta; perform manual extraction in the field","Uterine atony; perform fundal massage and consider oxytocin administration per protocol","Normal postpartum bleeding requiring no intervention","Placenta previa; avoid all uterine manipulation"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Uterine atony, failure of the uterus to contract adequately after delivery, is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage, and a soft, boggy fundus is the classic finding. Vigorous fundal massage stimulates uterine contraction and is the immediate first-line intervention, along with oxytocin administration if available under local protocol, to control bleeding." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5077", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the mechanism of action of oxytocin in the management of postpartum hemorrhage from uterine atony?",
  choices:["It directly constricts peripheral blood vessels","It stimulates uterine smooth muscle contraction, which compresses uterine blood vessels and reduces bleeding","It promotes clotting factor synthesis in the liver","It is a direct vasodilator that improves uterine perfusion"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Oxytocin stimulates rhythmic contraction of uterine smooth muscle, which mechanically compresses the spiral arteries that supplied the placental bed, reducing postpartum bleeding. This mechanical compression from uterine contraction, rather than a direct vascular or coagulation effect, is why uterine atony causes hemorrhage and why restoring tone controls it." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5078", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A newborn delivered at term is apneic, limp, and cyanotic. After drying, warming, positioning, and suctioning, the infant remains apneic with a heart rate of 90 bpm. What is the appropriate next step per the Neonatal Resuscitation Program?",
  choices:["Begin chest compressions immediately","Begin positive pressure ventilation with room air or blended oxygen, reassessing heart rate after 30 seconds","Administer epinephrine via the umbilical vein immediately","Intubate before attempting any bag-mask ventilation"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"After initial steps fail to establish spontaneous, effective respirations, positive pressure ventilation is indicated for a heart rate below 100 bpm or continued apnea, and heart rate should be reassessed after approximately 30 seconds of effective ventilation. Chest compressions are reserved for a heart rate below 60 bpm despite adequate PPV, and epinephrine is a later step if compressions with ventilation are insufficient." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5079", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"evaluation",
  question:"A newborn is receiving effective positive pressure ventilation and chest compressions. The heart rate remains below 60 bpm after adequate ventilation and compressions for 60 seconds. What is the appropriate next intervention?",
  choices:["Continue compressions and ventilation indefinitely with no further intervention","Administer epinephrine via the umbilical venous or intraosseous route","Perform immediate cardioversion","Discontinue resuscitation efforts"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"If the heart rate remains below 60 bpm despite 60 seconds of effective positive pressure ventilation and chest compressions with 100% oxygen, epinephrine should be administered, with the umbilical vein being the preferred route when accessible, or intraosseous access as an alternative. Cardioversion is not indicated in a bradycardic newborn without a shockable rhythm." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5080", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the recommended compression-to-ventilation ratio during neonatal resuscitation with a cardiac and respiratory indication for compressions?",
  choices:["30:2","15:2","3:1","5:1"],
  answerIndex:3,
  explanation:"Neonatal resuscitation uses a 3:1 compression-to-ventilation ratio (three compressions followed by one ventilation, achieving approximately 90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute), distinct from adult (30:2) or single-rescuer pediatric ratios, reflecting the predominantly respiratory etiology of most neonatal arrests." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5081", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 4-month-old infant presents with a barky cough, inspiratory stridor at rest, and mild retractions, with a low-grade fever. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Epiglottitis","Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)","Bacterial pneumonia","Foreign body aspiration"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Croup, typically caused by parainfluenza virus, classically presents with a barky/seal-like cough, inspiratory stridor, and a low-grade fever, often worse at night, in young children. Epiglottitis presents with a more toxic appearance, drooling, and reluctance to lie flat; the barky cough is a distinguishing feature favoring croup." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5082", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the appropriate prehospital treatment for a pediatric patient with moderate to severe croup and audible stridor at rest?",
  choices:["Nebulized racemic or L-epinephrine, and keep the child calm and in a position of comfort with a caregiver","Aggressive throat examination with a tongue depressor","Immediate intubation regardless of severity","Withhold treatment and transport without intervention"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Nebulized epinephrine (racemic or L-epinephrine) reduces airway mucosal edema through alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction, providing temporary relief in moderate to severe croup. Keeping the child calm, avoiding agitation (including aggressive throat exams), and allowing a position of comfort are important, since agitation and crying can worsen dynamic airway obstruction in croup." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5083", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 3-year-old presents with a high fever, drooling, a muffled voice, and a preference for sitting upright and leaning forward (tripod position), with a rapidly worsening course. What diagnosis should be strongly suspected, and what should be avoided?",
  choices:["Croup; nebulized epinephrine should be given immediately","Epiglottitis; avoid agitating the child or examining the airway/throat directly, which could precipitate complete obstruction","Asthma exacerbation; give albuterol immediately","Foreign body aspiration; perform back blows immediately"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Epiglottitis presents with a toxic-appearing child, high fever, drooling, muffled ('hot potato') voice, and a tripod positioning to maximize airway patency. Direct visualization or agitation of the airway can precipitate complete obstruction due to the severely inflamed epiglottis, so the priority is to keep the child calm, avoid unnecessary examination, and transport rapidly with airway equipment ready." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5084", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 2-month-old infant presents with tachypnea, wheezing, nasal flaring, and poor feeding, with a recent history of upper respiratory symptoms in an older sibling. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Bronchiolitis, most commonly caused by RSV","Asthma exacerbation","Pertussis","Congenital heart disease"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Bronchiolitis, most commonly caused by respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), typically affects infants under 2 years old and presents with wheezing, tachypnea, and respiratory distress following a viral prodrome, often with a history of exposure to an older sick sibling. True asthma is uncommon at this young age." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5085", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 6-month-old presents with a temperature of 104°F and then experiences a brief generalized tonic-clonic seizure lasting less than 5 minutes, with rapid return to baseline mental status afterward. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Status epilepticus","Simple febrile seizure","Meningitis-related seizure requiring lumbar puncture in the field","Epilepsy diagnosis confirmed"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A simple febrile seizure is a brief (under 15 minutes), generalized seizure occurring with fever in a child typically between 6 months and 5 years, with rapid return to normal mental status and no focal features. It is generally benign, though the underlying cause of fever should still be evaluated, and any prolonged or focal seizure warrants more concern for an alternative etiology." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5086", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Which formula is used to estimate the appropriate uncuffed endotracheal tube size for a child older than 2 years?",
  choices:["(Age in years / 4) + 4","(Weight in kg / 4) + 4","(Age in years x 2) + 4","(Height in cm / 4)"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"The formula (age in years / 4) + 4 is the standard estimate for uncuffed endotracheal tube internal diameter in children older than 2 years. Length-based resuscitation tapes are also commonly used in clinical practice to estimate tube size and weight-based medication doses." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5087", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A pediatric patient presents with lethargy, poor perfusion, and a capillary refill of 5 seconds, but a normal blood pressure for age. How should this finding be interpreted?",
  choices:["The patient is not in shock, since blood pressure is normal","This represents compensated shock; children can maintain normal blood pressure until late in shock through robust compensatory mechanisms","This indicates the patient is well hydrated","Normal blood pressure excludes any need for further intervention"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Children have strong compensatory mechanisms (tachycardia, vasoconstriction) that can maintain a normal blood pressure until shock is quite advanced, so hypotension is a late and ominous finding in pediatric shock. Signs like delayed capillary refill, tachycardia, and altered mental status must be recognized as early indicators of compensated shock, not dismissed because blood pressure appears normal." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5088", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues", scenarioId:"scenario-geripsych-001", scenarioStage:"scene",
  question:"You are dispatched to a residence for an 82-year-old male with acute confusion that began this morning, according to his daughter. He has a history of mild dementia but is normally oriented to person and place. On scene, he is agitated, inattentive, and his level of consciousness fluctuates throughout your assessment. Vitals reveal a temperature of 101.8°F and mild tachycardia. What is the most likely underlying process?",
  choices:["Progression of his baseline dementia","Delirium, likely secondary to an acute underlying illness such as infection","A normal sundowning pattern with no medical cause","Primary psychiatric decompensation"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Delirium is characterized by an acute onset, fluctuating course, and inattention, distinct from dementia's gradual, progressive decline in a stable level of consciousness. The presence of fever suggests an underlying acute illness (such as infection, e.g. urinary tract infection or pneumonia in the elderly) precipitating the delirium, which requires identification and treatment of the underlying cause rather than being dismissed as baseline dementia or a psychiatric issue." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5089", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues", scenarioId:"scenario-geripsych-001", scenarioStage:"post_scene",
  question:"Continuing with the same patient from the prior scenario, en route to the hospital his daughter mentions he takes several medications including a new antibiotic started three days ago for a urinary tract infection, along with his usual medications. Which consideration is most important regarding his presentation?",
  choices:["Medications are unlikely to contribute to delirium in the elderly","Polypharmacy and new medications are common and important precipitants of delirium in older adults and should be communicated to receiving providers","Only illicit drug use should be considered as a cause of delirium","The antibiotic should be discontinued immediately by the paramedic"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Older adults are particularly susceptible to medication-induced delirium due to altered pharmacokinetics, polypharmacy, and drug interactions; a new medication started around the time symptoms began is an important clue that should be relayed to the hospital. Paramedics do not discontinue prescribed medications, but documenting and reporting this history is a valuable clinical judgment contribution to the receiving team." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5090", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"An elderly patient with a history of atrial fibrillation on chronic anticoagulation falls and strikes his head. He is initially alert with a normal neurological exam but his family is concerned. What should guide the clinical decision-making regarding this patient's risk?",
  choices:["Anticoagulated patients have no increased risk of intracranial hemorrhage after head trauma","Anticoagulated patients are at significantly increased risk for delayed intracranial hemorrhage, even after seemingly minor trauma with an initially normal exam","Only patients with an initial loss of consciousness require any further evaluation","Anticoagulation only matters for extremity injuries, not head trauma"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Patients on anticoagulants (such as warfarin or direct oral anticoagulants) are at significantly elevated risk for intracranial hemorrhage after head trauma, and this risk exists even with minor mechanisms and an initially reassuring exam, since bleeding can be delayed in onset. This history should be clearly communicated and should lower the threshold for transport and evaluation regardless of initial presentation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5091", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A 78-year-old presents with vague complaints of generalized weakness and fatigue. Her vital signs show a heart rate of 48 bpm and blood pressure of 88/54 mmHg. What is an important consideration in geriatric assessment illustrated by this case?",
  choices:["Elderly patients always present with classic, textbook symptoms","Elderly patients often present atypically, with vague complaints rather than classic symptoms, requiring a broader differential and careful vital sign interpretation","Vital signs are unreliable in the elderly and should be disregarded","Weakness in the elderly is never a sign of a serious underlying condition"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Elderly patients frequently present atypically, with vague complaints such as generalized weakness rather than classic symptoms (chest pain, for example), which can delay recognition of serious conditions such as symptomatic bradycardia, myocardial infarction, or sepsis. This particular patient's bradycardia and hypotension warrant careful cardiac evaluation despite the nonspecific chief complaint." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5092", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe abdominal pain out of proportion to physical examination findings, along with a history of atrial fibrillation and recent onset of bloody diarrhea. What condition should be suspected?",
  choices:["Simple gastroenteritis","Acute mesenteric ischemia from embolic occlusion","Appendicitis","Peptic ulcer disease"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Acute mesenteric ischemia classically presents with severe abdominal pain that is disproportionate to the relatively benign examination findings early in the course, and atrial fibrillation is a significant risk factor due to the potential for embolic occlusion of a mesenteric vessel. This is a surgical emergency with high mortality if not recognized promptly." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5093", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 70-year-old male presents with sudden severe back and abdominal pain, a pulsatile abdominal mass, and hypotension. What diagnosis must be strongly suspected?",
  choices:["Renal colic","Ruptured or leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm","Simple constipation","Diverticulitis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A pulsatile abdominal mass with sudden severe back/abdominal pain and hypotension is highly concerning for a ruptured or leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm, a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate recognition, permissive hypotension resuscitation strategies, and rapid transport to a facility capable of emergency surgical intervention." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5094", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with a suspected ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm has a palpable radial pulse and a blood pressure of 84/50 mmHg. What is the appropriate fluid resuscitation strategy?",
  choices:["Aggressive fluid resuscitation to achieve a normal blood pressure","Permissive hypotension, administering fluids judiciously to maintain adequate perfusion (such as a palpable radial pulse) without normalizing blood pressure, to avoid disrupting a forming clot","Withhold all fluids regardless of perfusion status","Administer only vasopressors with no fluid at all"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In suspected uncontrolled internal hemorrhage such as a ruptured AAA, aggressive fluid resuscitation to a normal blood pressure can disrupt early clot formation and worsen bleeding. Permissive hypotension, targeting adequate perfusion (such as a palpable radial pulse and improving mentation) rather than a normal blood pressure, is the current evidence-based approach pending definitive surgical control." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5095", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of alcohol use disorder presents with confusion, ataxia, and abnormal eye movements (nystagmus/ophthalmoplegia). What deficiency should be suspected, and what treatment consideration is important?",
  choices:["Thiamine deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy); thiamine should be given before or with any glucose administration to avoid precipitating or worsening the condition","Vitamin C deficiency; no specific treatment consideration is needed","Iron deficiency; treatment is not urgent","Vitamin D deficiency; sunlight exposure is the treatment"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Wernicke encephalopathy, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency common in chronic alcohol use disorder, presents with the classic triad of confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus. Administering glucose to a thiamine-deficient patient without also providing thiamine can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy, since glucose metabolism consumes thiamine as a cofactor, so thiamine should be given concurrently or prior to glucose when feasible." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5096", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with chronic alcohol use disorder presents with tremors, tachycardia, hypertension, and visual/auditory hallucinations approximately 48 hours after their last drink. What condition is this presentation most consistent with?",
  choices:["Simple alcohol intoxication","Alcohol withdrawal, potentially progressing to delirium tremens","A primary psychotic disorder","Wernicke encephalopathy alone"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Alcohol withdrawal typically begins within 6-24 hours of the last drink and can progress over the following days, with severe withdrawal (delirium tremens) classically presenting around 48-96 hours with autonomic hyperactivity (tachycardia, hypertension, tremor), hallucinations, and confusion. This is a potentially life-threatening condition, and benzodiazepines are the mainstay of treatment." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5097", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"What is the primary pharmacologic treatment for alcohol withdrawal, including seizures associated with withdrawal?",
  choices:["Phenytoin","Benzodiazepines","Beta blockers as monotherapy","Antipsychotics as first-line therapy"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Benzodiazepines are the treatment of choice for alcohol withdrawal, since they act on the same GABA receptor system that alcohol affects, effectively substituting for alcohol's CNS depressant effect and preventing progression to severe withdrawal, seizures, or delirium tremens. Phenytoin is not effective for alcohol withdrawal seizures specifically." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5098", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"define_hypothesis",
  question:"A patient presents with acute onset of severe, unilateral flank pain radiating to the groin, with nausea and vomiting, but no fever and a benign abdominal exam. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Appendicitis","Renal colic (ureterolithiasis)","Ectopic pregnancy","Testicular torsion"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The classic presentation of renal colic from a ureteral stone is sudden, severe, colicky flank pain radiating toward the groin, often with associated nausea and vomiting, and typically without significant fever or peritoneal signs unless a complicating infection is present. The patient often appears restless, unable to find a comfortable position, in contrast to peritonitis where patients lie still." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5099", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 25-year-old female presents with sudden severe unilateral lower abdominal pain, nausea, and a history of a recent missed menstrual period, with a positive pregnancy test and vaginal spotting. What life-threatening condition must be excluded?",
  choices:["Simple ovarian cyst with no urgency","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Normal early pregnancy discomfort","Urinary tract infection"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Sudden severe unilateral abdominal pain with a positive pregnancy test and vaginal bleeding raises significant concern for ruptured ectopic pregnancy, a life-threatening hemorrhagic emergency requiring rapid recognition, aggressive management of shock if present, and emergent transport for surgical evaluation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5100", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A 22-year-old female presents with sudden severe right lower quadrant pain, nausea, and vomiting, with an examination revealing an adnexal mass on history from a prior ultrasound. What condition should be suspected?",
  choices:["Ovarian torsion","Simple menstrual cramps","Urinary tract infection","Gastroenteritis"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Ovarian torsion presents with sudden, severe, often unilateral lower abdominal pain with nausea and vomiting, and is more common in patients with an enlarged ovary or ovarian mass/cyst, which predisposes to twisting on its vascular pedicle. This is a time-sensitive surgical emergency, since prolonged torsion can compromise ovarian blood supply and viability." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5101", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of Addison's disease presents with severe weakness, hypotension, abdominal pain, and altered mental status following a recent illness. What is this presentation most consistent with?",
  choices:["Simple viral gastroenteritis","Addisonian (adrenal) crisis","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Cushing syndrome"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Addisonian crisis is an acute, life-threatening exacerbation of adrenal insufficiency, often precipitated by physiologic stress such as illness, presenting with profound weakness, hypotension refractory to fluids, abdominal pain, and altered mental status due to insufficient cortisol production. It requires prompt recognition, aggressive fluid resuscitation, and steroid administration per protocol." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5102", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of hyperthyroidism presents with fever, severe tachycardia, agitation, and altered mental status following a recent surgical procedure. What condition should be suspected?",
  choices:["Myxedema coma","Thyroid storm","Simple anxiety attack","Hypoglycemia"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Thyroid storm is a life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism, often precipitated by a stressor such as surgery, infection, or trauma, presenting with high fever, severe tachycardia (often with arrhythmia), agitation or altered mental status, and can progress to cardiovascular collapse if untreated. Beta blockers are often used to control the severe tachycardia and adrenergic symptoms in the prehospital/ED setting." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5103", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"An elderly patient with untreated hypothyroidism presents with severe hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, and a depressed level of consciousness. What condition should be suspected?",
  choices:["Thyroid storm","Myxedema coma","Simple hypothermia unrelated to thyroid disease","Adrenal crisis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Myxedema coma is a rare, life-threatening manifestation of severe, prolonged, untreated hypothyroidism, presenting with hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, and altered/depressed mental status. It carries a high mortality rate and requires supportive care (including cautious rewarming) alongside definitive thyroid hormone replacement at the hospital." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5104", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues", itemType:"multiple_response",
  question:"Which of the following are appropriate prehospital considerations when managing a patient with suspected diabetic ketoacidosis? (Select 3.)",
  choices:["Administer isotonic IV fluids to address dehydration","Monitor for signs of respiratory compensation (Kussmaul respirations)","Withhold all IV fluids to avoid worsening cerebral edema in every patient","Recognize the risk of hypokalemia once insulin therapy is initiated at the hospital","Assume the patient is intoxicated with alcohol due to a fruity breath odor and defer assessment"],
  correctIndices:[0,1,3],
  explanation:"Appropriate DKA management includes isotonic fluid resuscitation, monitoring the compensatory Kussmaul respiratory pattern, and anticipating that insulin therapy (typically initiated at the hospital) will shift potassium intracellularly, risking hypokalemia if not carefully monitored and replaced. Fluids should not be universally withheld (though caution regarding cerebral edema risk is relevant in pediatric DKA specifically), and a fruity breath odor should raise suspicion for DKA, not be dismissed as alcohol intoxication." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5105", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with sudden severe testicular pain, swelling, and nausea, with the affected testicle appearing higher-riding than the unaffected side. What diagnosis must be considered a time-sensitive emergency?",
  choices:["Epididymitis","Testicular torsion","Simple hydrocele","Varicocele"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Testicular torsion presents with sudden severe testicular pain, often with nausea/vomiting, and the affected testicle may appear higher-riding or horizontally oriented due to the twisting of the spermatic cord. This is a true surgical emergency, since testicular viability decreases rapidly with time, generally requiring detorsion within about 6 hours for the best chance of salvage." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5106", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of major depressive disorder presents expressing active suicidal ideation with a specific plan and access to means. What is the priority for the paramedic in this encounter?",
  choices:["Ignore the statement since verbal expression rarely indicates true risk","Take the statement seriously, ensure scene and patient safety, and facilitate transport for further evaluation, involving law enforcement or crisis resources as needed per protocol","Argue with the patient about the merits of their plan","Leave the patient alone to calm down without further engagement"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Any expression of suicidal ideation, particularly with a specific plan and access to means, should be taken seriously as a high-risk indicator requiring careful safety assessment, de-escalation, and facilitation of appropriate psychiatric evaluation and transport, involving additional resources such as law enforcement or a crisis team as needed per local protocol." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5107", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents in an acute manic episode with pressured speech, grandiosity, and significantly decreased need for sleep. Which underlying medical or toxicological causes should still be considered before assuming a purely psychiatric etiology?",
  choices:["No medical workup is ever needed for apparent psychiatric presentations","Thyroid dysfunction, substance intoxication (such as stimulants), and other organic causes should be considered and excluded when possible","Only a psychiatric history should be obtained, with no vital signs or physical assessment necessary","Mania is always purely psychiatric with no organic causes"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Even when a presentation appears classically psychiatric, organic and toxicological causes (thyroid dysfunction, stimulant intoxication, certain medications, or other medical conditions) can mimic or precipitate manic-like symptoms and should be considered as part of a thorough assessment, including vital signs and physical examination, rather than assuming a purely psychiatric cause without evaluation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5108", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues", itemType:"multiple_choice", graphic:{kind:"ecg", rhythm:"peakedT", alt:"Lead II ECG rhythm strip."},
  question:"The ECG strip shown demonstrates progressive changes over several minutes in a dialysis-dependent patient who missed their last two sessions. What does this progression most likely represent?",
  choices:["Progressive hypokalemia","Progressive, worsening hyperkalemia approaching a pre-terminal rhythm","Normal variant ECG changes with no clinical significance","Digoxin toxicity"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The progression from peaked T waves to QRS widening and a sine-wave pattern is the classic, ominous ECG evolution of worsening hyperkalemia, which can progress to ventricular fibrillation or asystole if untreated. A missed dialysis patient is at high risk for this presentation due to accumulated potassium, and immediate treatment (calcium, then measures to shift and eliminate potassium) is critical." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5109", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient presents with a severe asthma exacerbation and altered mental status with minimal air movement, unresponsive to nebulized bronchodilators and IV magnesium. What is the appropriate next step?",
  choices:["Continue to wait for medications to work with no further intervention","Prepare for aggressive airway management, potentially including intubation, recognizing this represents impending respiratory failure","Discharge from care since the patient is not in cardiac arrest","Administer additional sedation with no ventilatory support"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A patient with severe asthma exacerbation, altered mental status, and minimal air movement unresponsive to standard therapy is exhibiting signs of impending respiratory failure and requires preparation for advanced airway management, since delaying intervention in this deteriorating state risks respiratory arrest. Ventilating asthmatic patients requires caution regarding air trapping and breath-stacking, but airway control cannot be delayed when failure is imminent." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5110", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is a key ventilatory consideration when providing positive pressure ventilation to a patient with severe bronchospasm (such as status asthmaticus)?",
  choices:["Ventilate as rapidly as possible to maximize minute ventilation","Allow adequate expiratory time to minimize the risk of breath-stacking and dynamic hyperinflation (auto-PEEP)","Use the smallest possible tidal volumes with very rapid rates","No special consideration is needed compared to a normal patient"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In severe bronchospasm, airflow obstruction during exhalation can cause breath-stacking (dynamic hyperinflation, or auto-PEEP) if adequate time is not allowed for full exhalation between breaths, which can lead to barotrauma and hemodynamic compromise from increased intrathoracic pressure. Slower rates with longer expiratory time are recommended in this population." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5111", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with sudden onset of severe unilateral eye pain, blurred vision, halos around lights, nausea, and a fixed, mid-dilated pupil. What condition should be suspected?",
  choices:["Simple conjunctivitis","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Migraine headache","Corneal abrasion"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Acute angle-closure glaucoma presents with sudden severe unilateral eye pain, blurred vision, halos around lights, nausea/vomiting, and a fixed, mid-dilated pupil due to acutely elevated intraocular pressure. This is an ophthalmologic emergency requiring urgent transport, since delayed treatment can result in permanent vision loss." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5112", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of atrial fibrillation on warfarin presents with sudden onset of a cold, pale, pulseless right lower extremity with pain and paresthesia. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Simple deep vein thrombosis","Acute arterial occlusion (acute limb ischemia) from embolism","Compartment syndrome from trauma","Cellulitis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The 'six P's' of acute arterial occlusion (pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, poikilothermia, and paralysis) in a patient with a source of embolism such as atrial fibrillation strongly suggest acute limb ischemia from arterial embolic occlusion, a time-sensitive limb- and life-threatening emergency requiring rapid transport for possible embolectomy or thrombolysis." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5113", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with unilateral leg swelling, warmth, and tenderness along the course of a deep vein, with a recent history of prolonged immobilization after surgery. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what significant complication is this patient at risk for?",
  choices:["Cellulitis; risk of sepsis","Deep vein thrombosis; risk of pulmonary embolism if the clot embolizes","Simple muscle strain; no significant complication risk","Compartment syndrome; risk of permanent nerve damage"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Unilateral leg swelling, warmth, and tenderness with a risk factor for venous stasis (recent surgery/immobilization) is classic for deep vein thrombosis. The major life-threatening complication is pulmonary embolism if a portion of the clot dislodges and travels to the pulmonary circulation, which should be considered in any DVT patient who develops sudden dyspnea or chest pain." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5114", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with cirrhosis presents with massive hematemesis (vomiting bright red blood) and hypotension. What is the most likely source of bleeding?",
  choices:["Peptic ulcer disease","Esophageal varices from portal hypertension","Mallory-Weiss tear","Gastritis alone"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In a patient with known cirrhosis, esophageal varices (dilated submucosal veins resulting from portal hypertension) are a leading cause of massive, life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage. This is a critical emergency with no definitive field treatment, requiring aggressive airway protection given the risk of aspiration, fluid resuscitation, and rapid transport for endoscopic or other hospital-based intervention." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5115", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with suspected esophageal variceal hemorrhage has ongoing massive hematemesis and a declining level of consciousness. What is the priority prehospital intervention?",
  choices:["Immediate administration of oral antacids","Aggressive airway management/protection given the high aspiration risk, along with IV fluid resuscitation and rapid transport","Delaying transport to attempt to control bleeding in the field with direct pressure","Administering activated charcoal"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Massive hematemesis with declining mental status poses a severe aspiration risk, making airway protection (positioning, suction, and potentially advanced airway management) a priority alongside fluid resuscitation for hemorrhagic shock. There is no field mechanism to directly control internal variceal bleeding, so rapid transport for definitive hospital-based intervention (such as endoscopic band ligation) is essential." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5116", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe, constant epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and vomiting, with a history of heavy alcohol use and elevated pain worsened by eating. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Simple gastritis","Acute pancreatitis","Appendicitis","Peptic ulcer perforation with no other findings"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Acute pancreatitis classically presents with severe, constant epigastric pain that radiates to the back, often worsened by eating and improved by leaning forward, with nausea and vomiting. Alcohol use and gallstones are the two most common causes. Management includes IV fluid resuscitation, analgesia, and antiemetics as needed, with transport for further evaluation and treatment of the underlying cause." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5117", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient with acute pancreatitis develops signs of hypocalcemia, including perioral tingling and muscle cramping. What is the mechanism behind this electrolyte abnormality in pancreatitis?",
  choices:["Increased renal calcium excretion only","Saponification, where calcium binds to fatty acids released by pancreatic lipase-mediated fat necrosis, lowering serum calcium","Decreased parathyroid hormone secretion caused directly by inflammation","Excessive calcium loss through vomiting alone"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In severe pancreatitis, released pancreatic lipase causes fat necrosis, and the resulting free fatty acids bind calcium in a process called saponification, sequestering it and lowering serum calcium levels. This can be significant enough to cause symptomatic hypocalcemia, including neuromuscular irritability findings such as perioral tingling, muscle cramps, or Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5118", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with colicky abdominal pain, distension, vomiting, and obstipation (no stool or flatus passage). Abdominal x-ray at the hospital confirms bowel obstruction. What is a key prehospital management priority?",
  choices:["Encourage oral intake to help move the obstruction along","Manage nausea/vomiting, monitor for signs of dehydration or shock, and avoid oral intake given the risk of aspiration and worsening distension","No fluid management is ever needed","Administer a large dose of a laxative in the field"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"In bowel obstruction, oral intake should be avoided due to the risk of vomiting/aspiration and worsening distension, and management focuses on antiemetic therapy, monitoring for and treating dehydration or shock (from third-spacing of fluid and vomiting), and supportive transport, since definitive treatment (which may be surgical or involve nasogastric decompression) occurs at the hospital." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5119", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"An infant presents with intermittent episodes of severe abdominal pain with drawing up of the legs, alternating with periods of apparent comfort, and 'currant jelly' stool. What diagnosis should be suspected?",
  choices:["Simple colic","Intussusception","Pyloric stenosis","Appendicitis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Intussusception, where a segment of bowel telescopes into an adjacent segment, classically presents in infants with episodic severe abdominal pain (with legs drawn up during episodes, comfortable between episodes), and 'currant jelly' stool from mixed blood and mucus, and requires urgent surgical or radiologic (enema) reduction to prevent bowel ischemia." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5120", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with a groin bulge that is tender, firm, and cannot be reduced (pushed back in), with associated nausea and abdominal pain. What complication of a hernia does this presentation suggest, and what is appropriate field management?",
  choices:["A reducible hernia; the paramedic should attempt manual reduction","An incarcerated, possibly strangulated hernia; do not attempt reduction, since this risks returning nonviable bowel into the abdomen","A simple muscle strain requiring no special precautions","Testicular torsion; ice should be applied directly to the area"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A tender, firm, irreducible hernia with associated symptoms raises concern for incarceration with possible strangulation (compromised blood supply to the herniated bowel). Reduction should NOT be attempted in the field in this setting, since forcing potentially nonviable, ischemic bowel back into the abdomen can be harmful; the priority is recognition, pain management, and transport for surgical evaluation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5121", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues", itemType:"build_list",
  question:"Place the following steps of the Neonatal Resuscitation Program algorithm in the correct sequential order for a depressed newborn.",
  steps:["Dry, warm, stimulate, and position the airway","Assess respirations and heart rate","Begin positive pressure ventilation if apneic or heart rate is below 100 bpm","Begin chest compressions if heart rate remains below 60 bpm despite effective PPV","Administer epinephrine if heart rate remains below 60 bpm despite compressions and ventilation"],
  correctOrder:[0,1,2,3,4],
  explanation:"The Neonatal Resuscitation Program follows a clear stepwise algorithm: initial warming/drying/stimulation/positioning, then assessment of respiratory effort and heart rate, escalating to positive pressure ventilation for inadequate breathing or bradycardia, then chest compressions for persistent severe bradycardia despite ventilation, and finally epinephrine if bradycardia persists despite compressions and ventilation. Each step is only escalated to after the prior step has failed to correct the problem." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5122", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"What is the recommended initial fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO2) for positive pressure ventilation of a term newborn during resuscitation?",
  choices:["100% oxygen","21% oxygen (room air)","50% oxygen","30% oxygen"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Current neonatal resuscitation guidelines recommend beginning positive pressure ventilation in term infants with room air (21% oxygen) and titrating oxygen concentration based on pulse oximetry targets, since excessive oxygen exposure in newborns has been associated with oxidative stress and other adverse outcomes." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5123", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with a sudden onset of a severe, one-sided, throbbing headache with photophobia, phonophobia, nausea, and a visual aura preceding the headache. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Migraine with aura","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Tension headache","Cluster headache"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Migraine with aura presents with a unilateral, throbbing headache accompanied by photophobia, phonophobia, and nausea, often preceded by a transient neurological aura (commonly visual disturbances such as scintillating scotomas). While migraine is generally benign, a sudden severe headache should still be assessed carefully to exclude more dangerous causes such as subarachnoid hemorrhage, particularly if it is the 'worst headache of life' or has an atypical pattern." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5124", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe, sharp, unilateral periorbital headache with associated ipsilateral tearing, nasal congestion, and eyelid drooping, occurring in clusters over several weeks. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Cluster headache","Tension headache","Migraine without aura","Trigeminal neuralgia"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Cluster headaches present with severe, sharp, unilateral periorbital or temporal pain, often accompanied by ipsilateral autonomic symptoms such as lacrimation, nasal congestion, and ptosis/miosis (partial Horner's syndrome), occurring in clusters of frequent attacks over weeks to months, then remission. High-flow oxygen is a well-documented effective treatment for acute cluster headache attacks." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5125", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with acute vertigo, nausea, and nystagmus. Which finding would suggest a central (rather than peripheral) cause of vertigo, warranting more urgent evaluation for stroke?",
  choices:["Vertigo that worsens with specific head position changes","Associated focal neurological deficits, such as diplopia, dysarthria, or limb ataxia","Nausea and vomiting alone","Symptoms lasting only a few seconds with positional triggers"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Central causes of vertigo (such as a posterior circulation stroke or cerebellar lesion) are more likely to present with additional focal neurological findings such as diplopia, dysarthria, ataxia, or other cranial nerve/cerebellar signs, whereas peripheral vertigo (such as benign paroxysmal positional vertigo or vestibular neuritis) typically presents with isolated vertigo, nausea, and nystagmus without other focal deficits. Any concerning focal findings should prompt treatment as a possible stroke." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5126", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with rapidly progressive, ascending symmetric weakness beginning in the lower extremities, following a recent gastrointestinal illness. What condition should be suspected, and what is a critical concern?",
  choices:["Bell's palsy; no respiratory concern exists","Guillain-Barre syndrome; risk of progression to respiratory muscle weakness and failure requiring close respiratory monitoring","Simple viral myalgia; no further monitoring needed","Myasthenia gravis; no relationship to infection exists"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Guillain-Barre syndrome is an autoimmune polyneuropathy often triggered by a preceding infection (commonly gastrointestinal, such as Campylobacter), presenting with ascending, symmetric weakness. A critical concern is progression to respiratory muscle involvement, which can lead to respiratory failure requiring ventilatory support, so respiratory status must be closely monitored throughout the encounter." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5127", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with known myasthenia gravis presents with acute worsening of generalized weakness, difficulty swallowing, and respiratory distress. What is this presentation concerning for, and what should be a priority?",
  choices:["A simple exacerbation requiring no urgency","Myasthenic crisis, with close monitoring of respiratory status and readiness for ventilatory support given the risk of respiratory failure","A stroke, requiring fibrinolytic administration in the field","Simple fatigue with no medical significance"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Myasthenic crisis is a severe exacerbation of myasthenia gravis involving significant weakness of the respiratory and bulbar (swallowing) muscles, which can progress rapidly to respiratory failure. Close monitoring of respiratory effort, oxygenation, and readiness to support ventilation are critical priorities, since these patients can decompensate quickly and require urgent transport." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5128", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with acute unilateral facial droop involving the forehead (unable to raise the eyebrow on the affected side), without limb weakness or other focal neurological deficits. What is this presentation most consistent with?",
  choices:["Ischemic stroke, since any facial droop indicates stroke","Bell's palsy, a peripheral facial nerve (CN VII) palsy that typically involves the forehead, unlike a central (stroke-related) facial droop which spares forehead movement","Simple anxiety with no organic cause","Trigeminal neuralgia"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Bell's palsy, a peripheral facial nerve palsy, classically involves the entire side of the face including forehead movement, since the peripheral nerve controls all facial muscles on that side. A central (stroke-related) facial droop typically spares the forehead due to bilateral cortical innervation to the upper facial muscles, so forehead involvement is a key distinguishing feature favoring a peripheral rather than central cause, though any acute facial weakness still warrants careful assessment to exclude stroke." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5129", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with fever, severe headache, photophobia, and nuchal rigidity (neck stiffness). What diagnosis must be considered, and what is an important prehospital management consideration?",
  choices:["Simple viral upper respiratory infection; no special precautions needed","Meningitis; use of appropriate personal protective equipment (mask) is important given the potential for bacterial meningitis to be transmitted via respiratory droplets","Migraine; no special precautions needed","Sinusitis; treat with decongestants only"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The classic triad of fever, severe headache, and nuchal rigidity should raise strong concern for meningitis. Bacterial meningitis in particular can be transmitted via respiratory droplets, so appropriate PPE (including a mask) for the crew is an important consideration, along with prompt recognition and rapid transport, since bacterial meningitis can progress rapidly and is potentially fatal without timely antibiotic treatment." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5130", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of chronic kidney disease and elevated ammonia levels from liver failure presents with confusion, asterixis (flapping tremor), and lethargy. What condition is this presentation most consistent with?",
  choices:["Simple uremic fatigue","Hepatic encephalopathy","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Wernicke encephalopathy"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Hepatic encephalopathy results from the accumulation of neurotoxic substances (particularly ammonia) that the diseased liver can no longer adequately clear, presenting with a spectrum from mild confusion to coma, often accompanied by asterixis (a characteristic flapping tremor when the wrists are extended). It occurs in the setting of significant liver dysfunction, such as cirrhosis." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5131", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with acute confusion, tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry flushed skin, urinary retention, and absent bowel sounds after a suspected ingestion. What toxidrome does this presentation most closely match?",
  choices:["Cholinergic toxidrome","Anticholinergic toxidrome ('red as a beet, dry as a bone, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare')","Opioid toxidrome","Sympathomimetic toxidrome with normal skin findings"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The anticholinergic toxidrome, classically remembered by the mnemonic 'red as a beet (flushed skin), dry as a bone (dry mucous membranes/skin), blind as a bat (mydriasis, blurred vision), mad as a hatter (confusion/delirium), and hot as a hare (hyperthermia),' along with urinary retention and decreased bowel sounds, results from blockade of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, seen with agents like diphenhydramine, TCAs, and certain plant toxins." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5132", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with confusion, miosis (pinpoint pupils), significant bradypnea, and track marks on both arms. What toxidrome and likely cause does this suggest?",
  choices:["Anticholinergic toxidrome from antihistamine overdose","Opioid toxidrome, likely from injection drug use","Sympathomimetic toxidrome from stimulant use","Cholinergic toxidrome from pesticide exposure"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Miosis (pinpoint pupils), significant respiratory depression, and altered mental status are classic for the opioid toxidrome, and track marks suggest a history of injection drug use as a likely route of exposure. This presentation should prompt consideration of naloxone administration alongside ventilatory support as needed." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5133", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with agitation, tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, and dilated pupils after suspected use of methamphetamine. What toxidrome does this represent?",
  choices:["Opioid toxidrome","Sympathomimetic toxidrome","Cholinergic toxidrome","Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"The sympathomimetic toxidrome, seen with agents like methamphetamine, cocaine, and other stimulants, presents with agitation, tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, and mydriasis (dilated pupils), reflecting excess sympathetic nervous system activity. This overlaps significantly with the anticholinergic toxidrome, but sympathomimetic toxicity typically presents with diaphoretic (moist), not dry, skin, which helps distinguish the two." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5134", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with suspected acetaminophen overdose is being transported to the hospital. What is important prehospital information to obtain and relay to the receiving facility?",
  choices:["The specific time of ingestion and the estimated amount ingested, since this determines the timing and utility of N-acetylcysteine treatment based on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram","Acetaminophen overdose has no clinically significant risk and requires no special history","Only the patient's current vital signs matter, not ingestion timing","Activated charcoal should always be withheld regardless of timing"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Acetaminophen overdose can cause severe, delayed hepatotoxicity, and the timing of the overdose relative to a blood level (interpreted via the Rumack-Matthew nomogram) determines whether and when N-acetylcysteine treatment is indicated. Obtaining and relaying an accurate history of ingestion time and amount is therefore critical prehospital information, even though the patient may appear asymptomatic initially, since early symptoms can be minimal despite significant risk of later liver injury." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5135", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with tinnitus, hyperventilation, nausea, and vomiting after a suspected large ingestion of aspirin. What acid-base disturbance is classically seen early in salicylate toxicity?",
  choices:["Pure metabolic acidosis only","A mixed respiratory alkalosis (from direct respiratory center stimulation) and metabolic acidosis (from uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation)","Pure respiratory acidosis","Pure metabolic alkalosis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Salicylate toxicity classically produces a mixed acid-base picture: early respiratory alkalosis from direct stimulation of the medullary respiratory center causing hyperventilation, combined with an underlying metabolic acidosis from uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation and accumulation of organic acids. Tinnitus is a classic early symptom of salicylate toxicity." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5136", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with irritability, tremor, and mild tachycardia approximately 8 hours after their last dose of a prescribed opioid pain medication, with no significant hemodynamic instability. What is the most likely explanation?",
  choices:["Opioid overdose","Opioid withdrawal","Anticholinergic toxicity","Sympathomimetic toxicity"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Opioid withdrawal presents with symptoms such as irritability, tremor, tachycardia, piloerection, yawning, lacrimation, rhinorrhea, and abdominal cramping, occurring after a period without the drug in a physically dependent patient. While profoundly uncomfortable, opioid withdrawal is generally not life-threatening (unlike opioid overdose or alcohol/benzodiazepine withdrawal), and supportive care with transport for further management is appropriate." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5137", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents after ingesting a caustic household chemical, with oral burns, drooling, and odynophagia (painful swallowing). What is an important prehospital consideration in managing this patient?",
  choices:["Induce vomiting immediately to remove the substance","Avoid inducing vomiting, since re-exposure of the esophagus to the caustic substance can worsen injury, and avoid attempts to neutralize the substance in the field","Administer activated charcoal immediately","Give the patient large amounts of water to dilute the chemical by mouth regardless of airway status"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"With caustic ingestions, inducing vomiting is contraindicated because it re-exposes the esophagus and oropharynx to the caustic agent, worsening tissue injury, and can increase aspiration risk. Neutralizing agents are also avoided since the resulting exothermic reaction can cause additional thermal injury. Airway status must be closely monitored given the risk of progressive edema; activated charcoal is not useful for caustic ingestions and does not bind corrosive agents effectively." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5138", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues", itemType:"multiple_response",
  question:"Which of the following findings are consistent with a diagnosis of preeclampsia in a pregnant patient after 20 weeks gestation? (Select 3.)",
  choices:["Blood pressure of 145/95 mmHg on two occasions", "Significant new-onset proteinuria", "New-onset headache and visual disturbances", "A blood pressure of 118/76 mmHg", "Normal reflexes with no other symptoms"],
  correctIndices:[0,1,2],
  explanation:"Preeclampsia is diagnosed based on new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks gestation (typically defined as sustained blood pressure at or above 140/90) combined with evidence of end-organ involvement, such as significant proteinuria or symptoms like headache, visual disturbances, or epigastric pain. A normal blood pressure or normal findings with no other symptoms would not support this diagnosis." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5139", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A postpartum patient approximately 5 days after delivery presents with fever, uterine tenderness, and foul-smelling lochia (vaginal discharge). What condition should be suspected?",
  choices:["Normal postpartum recovery","Postpartum endometritis (uterine infection)","Mastitis","Simple urinary tract infection"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Postpartum endometritis presents with fever, uterine tenderness, and malodorous lochia in the days following delivery, representing an infection of the uterine lining. It requires antibiotic treatment and can progress to more severe infection (such as sepsis) if untreated, so prompt recognition and transport for evaluation are important." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5140", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"During a precipitous field delivery, the baby's shoulders become stuck after the head delivers ('turtle sign,' the head retracting against the perineum). What complication is this, and what is an appropriate field maneuver?",
  choices:["Normal delivery variant requiring no special maneuvers","Shoulder dystocia; apply the McRoberts maneuver (hyperflexing the mother's hips toward her chest) and suprapubic pressure","Breech presentation; attempt to deliver the legs first","Cord prolapse; push the presenting part back"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Shoulder dystocia occurs when the fetal shoulders become impacted behind the maternal pubic symphysis after the head delivers, creating an obstetric emergency due to risk of cord compression and fetal hypoxia. The McRoberts maneuver (sharply flexing the mother's thighs toward her abdomen) combined with suprapubic pressure (not fundal pressure, which can worsen impaction) is the recommended first-line field maneuver to help resolve the obstruction." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5141", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:false,
  question:"Why is fundal pressure specifically avoided when managing shoulder dystocia during delivery?",
  choices:["Fundal pressure has no effect on the fetus","Fundal pressure can further impact the anterior shoulder against the pubic symphysis, worsening the obstruction and increasing risk of injury","Fundal pressure always causes immediate uterine rupture","Fundal pressure is only contraindicated in twin pregnancies"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Applying pressure to the fundus (top of the uterus) during shoulder dystocia can push the already-impacted anterior shoulder further against the maternal pubic symphysis, worsening the obstruction rather than relieving it, and increasing the risk of brachial plexus injury or other fetal trauma. Suprapubic pressure, applied differently, is used instead to help dislodge the impacted shoulder." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5142", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A newborn delivered with thick, particulate meconium-stained amniotic fluid is vigorous, crying, and has good tone and a normal heart rate immediately after birth. What is the appropriate management?",
  choices:["Immediate endotracheal suctioning of meconium below the vocal cords in all cases","Routine care, including drying, warming, and stimulation, without endotracheal suctioning, since the infant is vigorous","No monitoring is needed regardless of appearance","Suction meconium using a bulb syringe only in the oropharynx of a vigorous infant, then proceed with routine care as clinically indicated"],
  answerIndex:3,
  explanation:"Current Neonatal Resuscitation Program guidance no longer recommends routine endotracheal suctioning of meconium in a vigorous newborn (good tone, strong respiratory effort, heart rate above 100), even with meconium-stained fluid; routine care with gentle clearing of the oropharynx as needed is appropriate for a vigorous infant, while a non-vigorous infant may warrant more targeted airway clearing based on current protocols and clinical judgment." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5143", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A pregnant patient at 38 weeks gestation reports regular, increasingly painful contractions every 3-4 minutes lasting 45-60 seconds, and states she feels the urge to push. What is the most appropriate action?",
  choices:["Transport immediately regardless of assessment, delaying any exam","Perform a brief assessment to determine if delivery is imminent (crowning), and prepare for field delivery if birth appears imminent rather than attempting transport","Administer tocolytic medication to stop labor","Instruct the patient to cross her legs to delay delivery until hospital arrival"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"When a patient reports frequent, strong contractions and an urge to push, a brief assessment for crowning (visualization of the fetal head at the vaginal opening) should be performed to determine if delivery is imminent. If crowning is present, preparation for field delivery is appropriate rather than attempting transport, since attempting to delay or transport during imminent delivery risks an uncontrolled delivery en route." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5144", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"Immediately after delivery, a newborn is not crying, has poor tone, and a heart rate of 70 bpm despite adequate drying and stimulation. What is the priority action?",
  choices:["Continue drying and stimulation only, with no further intervention","Begin positive pressure ventilation immediately","Begin chest compressions immediately without ventilation first","Delay any intervention for 5 minutes to allow spontaneous improvement"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A heart rate below 100 bpm with inadequate respiratory effort despite initial stimulation is an indication to begin positive pressure ventilation without further delay, per the Neonatal Resuscitation Program algorithm. Chest compressions would only be indicated after a period of effective PPV if the heart rate remains below 60 bpm, not as an initial step before attempting ventilation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5145", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A pregnant trauma patient at 30 weeks gestation is involved in a motor vehicle collision. She is hemodynamically stable, but the paramedic notes uterine contractions and vaginal bleeding on assessment. What complication should be suspected?",
  choices:["Normal Braxton Hicks contractions with no significant concern","Placental abruption secondary to the traumatic mechanism","A urinary tract infection unrelated to the trauma","Round ligament pain from the seatbelt"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Blunt abdominal trauma in pregnancy can cause placental abruption even without other obvious signs of major injury, and the combination of new uterine contractions and vaginal bleeding after trauma should raise strong suspicion for this potentially life-threatening complication for both mother and fetus, warranting close monitoring and rapid transport to an appropriate facility." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5146", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with jaundice, right upper quadrant pain, and fever after a fatty meal, with a positive Murphy's sign on examination. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Acute cholecystitis","Acute pancreatitis","Appendicitis","Peptic ulcer disease"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Acute cholecystitis, inflammation of the gallbladder typically from gallstone obstruction of the cystic duct, classically presents with right upper quadrant pain (often after a fatty meal), fever, and a positive Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest with palpation of the right upper quadrant due to pain). Jaundice can occur if there is associated bile duct obstruction. Management includes analgesia, antiemetics, and transport for surgical evaluation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5147", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of atrial fibrillation presents with a sudden episode of transient monocular vision loss described as a 'curtain coming down' over one eye, which resolved completely within 30 minutes. What condition does this represent?",
  choices:["A simple migraine with visual aura","Amaurosis fugax, a form of transient ischemic attack from retinal artery embolism, warranting the same urgency as any TIA","Simple eye strain","Conjunctivitis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Amaurosis fugax describes transient monocular vision loss, classically described as a curtain descending, caused by transient occlusion of the retinal artery, often embolic in origin (particularly relevant given the atrial fibrillation history). It represents a form of transient ischemic attack and should be treated with the same urgency as any TIA, given the significant risk of subsequent stroke." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5148", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action", itemType:"options_table",
  question:"For each medication listed, identify whether it is appropriate as a first-line treatment for status epilepticus or should be reserved as a second-line/adjunct agent in the prehospital setting.",
  options:["First-line","Second-line/adjunct"],
  rows:[
    { id:"r1", finding:"Midazolam IM/IN/IV", correctOptionIndex:0 },
    { id:"r2", finding:"Lorazepam IV", correctOptionIndex:0 },
    { id:"r3", finding:"Levetiracetam IV", correctOptionIndex:1 },
    { id:"r4", finding:"Magnesium sulfate for a non-eclamptic seizure", correctOptionIndex:1 }
  ],
  explanation:"Benzodiazepines (midazolam, lorazepam, diazepam) are the established first-line prehospital treatment for status epilepticus, since they rapidly enhance GABAergic inhibition. Second-line agents such as levetiracetam or fosphenytoin are typically reserved for refractory status epilepticus and are less commonly carried or indicated as first prehospital treatment. Magnesium is specifically indicated for eclamptic seizures in pregnancy, not general seizure disorders." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5149", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease presents in significant respiratory distress with a productive cough of purulent sputum, fever, and increased work of breathing beyond their baseline. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["A simple viral cold with no significant change from baseline","COPD exacerbation, likely triggered by an infectious process","Pulmonary embolism with no infectious component","Anaphylaxis"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"An acute COPD exacerbation presents with worsening dyspnea, increased sputum production (often purulent, suggesting an infectious trigger), and increased work of breathing beyond the patient's chronic baseline. Bronchodilators (albuterol/ipratropium), and sometimes steroids, are mainstays of treatment, along with careful oxygen titration given the risk in some chronic CO2 retainers." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5150", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with sudden-onset unilateral pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea, with decreased breath sounds and hyperresonance to percussion on the affected side, but no history of trauma. Vital signs are stable. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  choices:["Tension pneumothorax","Spontaneous pneumothorax","Massive pulmonary embolism","Acute myocardial infarction"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"A spontaneous pneumothorax, often occurring in tall, thin young males or those with underlying lung disease, presents with sudden unilateral pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, decreased breath sounds, and hyperresonance on the affected side. Unlike tension pneumothorax, hemodynamics remain stable, since there is no progressive increase in intrathoracic pressure causing mediastinal shift and cardiovascular compromise." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5151", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"analyze_cues",
  question:"A patient with a spontaneous pneumothorax develops progressively worsening dyspnea, hypotension, jugular venous distension, and tracheal deviation away from the affected side. What has this progressed to, and what is the appropriate treatment?",
  choices:["Simple pneumothorax; continue supportive care and monitoring only","Tension pneumothorax; perform needle decompression at the appropriate anatomic landmark","Cardiac tamponade; perform pericardiocentesis","Massive hemothorax; administer blood products only"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Progressive respiratory distress, hypotension, JVD, and tracheal deviation indicate progression to tension pneumothorax, where accumulating intrapleural pressure causes mediastinal shift and impairs venous return, producing obstructive shock. This requires immediate needle decompression at the second intercostal space, midclavicular line (or the fifth intercostal space, anterior/midaxillary line per updated guidance) to relieve the pressure emergently." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5152", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient with known chronic renal failure on dialysis missed their last two scheduled sessions and now presents with confusion, muscle weakness, and peaked T waves on the monitor. What is the most likely underlying electrolyte abnormality?",
  choices:["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyponatremia"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Missed dialysis sessions lead to accumulation of potassium (which is normally cleared by dialysis in a patient with kidney failure), producing hyperkalemia, which manifests with muscle weakness, confusion, and characteristic ECG changes progressing from peaked T waves toward more dangerous rhythm disturbances if untreated. This is a true emergency requiring prompt recognition and treatment." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5153", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient with severe symptomatic hyponatremia presents with seizure activity. What is an important consideration regarding correction of sodium in this setting?",
  choices:["Sodium should be corrected as rapidly as possible with no upper limit of concern","Overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome; correction should generally be gradual except in acute, severely symptomatic cases requiring hospital-guided management","Sodium correction has no associated risks regardless of rate","Only oral sodium replacement should ever be used"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia carries a risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome (previously called central pontine myelinolysis), a serious and potentially permanent neurological injury. While severely symptomatic acute hyponatremia (such as with seizures) may require more urgent correction under careful hospital-based monitoring, the general principle is that correction should not be excessively rapid, and this is an important consideration paramedics should be aware of when transporting such patients." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5154", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with generalized edema, significant proteinuria found on prior lab work, and a history of recent significant weight gain from fluid retention, without primary cardiac or hepatic disease. What condition might this presentation suggest?",
  choices:["Nephrotic syndrome","Simple dietary sodium excess","Hyperthyroidism","Addison's disease"],
  answerIndex:0,
  explanation:"Nephrotic syndrome results from a glomerular disease process causing significant protein loss in the urine, leading to hypoalbuminemia and resultant generalized edema (including periorbital and dependent edema) and fluid retention, distinct from cardiac or hepatic causes of edema. While the specific diagnosis is made with laboratory and other testing beyond prehospital capability, recognizing the pattern helps guide appropriate transport and communication with receiving providers." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5155", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with severe, colicky right upper quadrant pain radiating to the right scapula, occurring after eating a large fatty meal, without fever or jaundice. What is this presentation most consistent with?",
  choices:["Acute cholecystitis with infection","Biliary colic from gallstones without cholecystitis","Acute pancreatitis","Peptic ulcer perforation"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Biliary colic describes pain from transient obstruction of the cystic duct by a gallstone, typically producing colicky right upper quadrant pain radiating to the right scapula or shoulder after a fatty meal, without the fever or systemic signs of infection seen in cholecystitis (which represents true inflammation, often with a positive Murphy's sign and possible fever). Both conditions warrant analgesia and transport for further evaluation." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5156", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"take_action",
  question:"A patient in cardiac arrest is found to have a rhythm of torsades de pointes that degenerates into pulseless ventricular fibrillation. What is the appropriate immediate treatment in addition to standard ACLS defibrillation and CPR?",
  choices:["Administer amiodarone as the sole antiarrhythmic with no other consideration","Administer magnesium sulfate IV push in addition to standard defibrillation and CPR, given the known association with torsades","Withhold all antiarrhythmics since the patient is now pulseless","Administer atropine"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"When torsades de pointes degenerates into cardiac arrest with pulseless VT/VF, magnesium sulfate should be administered in addition to standard ACLS defibrillation and CPR, given the strong association between torsades and hypomagnesemia/QT prolongation, and its established role in both treating and preventing recurrence of this specific rhythm." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5157", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"recognize_cues",
  question:"A patient presents with progressive muscle weakness, hyporeflexia, and a flattened T wave with a prominent U wave on the ECG, with a history of chronic diuretic use and poor oral intake. What electrolyte abnormality is most likely?",
  choices:["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypernatremia"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Chronic diuretic use (particularly loop and thiazide diuretics) is a common cause of hypokalemia, which presents with muscle weakness, hyporeflexia, and characteristic ECG findings including flattened T waves and prominent U waves. Severe hypokalemia can also predispose to dangerous arrhythmias, including torsades de pointes." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5158", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"evaluation",
  question:"After administering IV fluids and calcium chloride to a hyperkalemic patient with widened QRS complexes, the paramedic reassesses the ECG and finds the QRS has narrowed and the rhythm has organized. What does this indicate?",
  choices:["The treatment has failed and should be repeated at a higher dose immediately without reassessment","The treatment is producing the intended membrane-stabilizing effect, and continued monitoring and further potassium-lowering measures per protocol are appropriate","The patient's serum potassium level has definitely normalized","No further treatment or monitoring is needed"],
  answerIndex:1,
  explanation:"Narrowing of the QRS complex and organization of the rhythm after calcium administration indicates the intended cardiac membrane-stabilizing effect is working, though it's important to recognize this does not lower the actual serum potassium level; continued monitoring, transport, and additional potassium-shifting measures (such as albuterol, per protocol) remain appropriate since the underlying hyperkalemia has not yet been corrected." },
{ id:"paramedic-medical-5159", domain:"Medical + OBGYN", level:"Paramedic", clinicalJudgment:true, clinicalJudgmentStep:"generate_solutions", itemType:"multiple_response",
  question:"A patient presents with signs of both cardiogenic shock (hypotension, poor perfusion, pulmonary edema) following a large anterior STEMI. Which prehospital management strategies are appropriate? (Select 2.)",
  choices:["Cautious fluid administration with close monitoring, since aggressive fluids can worsen pulmonary edema in cardiogenic shock","Administration of nitroglycerin at standard chest-pain doses regardless of blood pressure","Consideration of vasopressor/inotropic support per protocol if hypotension is severe and unresponsive to cautious interventions","Withholding oxygen even if the patient is significantly hypoxic","Delaying transport to administer a large empiric crystalloid bolus"],
  correctIndices:[0,2],
  explanation:"In cardiogenic shock, fluid administration must be cautious since the underlying problem is pump failure, not volume depletion, and aggressive fluids can worsen pulmonary edema; vasopressor or inotropic support may be needed per protocol if hypotension is severe. Nitroglycerin at standard doses would be inappropriate and potentially dangerous in a hypotensive cardiogenic shock patient, and oxygen should not be withheld from a hypoxic patient." }
];
